WEBVTT

00:00:11.950 --> 00:00:18.880
<v Ira Glass>In Ukraine, the government has set up this phone line. It's for soldiers to call-- Russian soldiers-- if they want to surrender.
<v Ira Glass>在乌克兰，政府设立了一条电话热线。这是供士兵——俄罗斯士兵——如果他们想投降时拨打的。

00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:21.110
<v Ira Glass>[CONNECTING TONE]
<v Ira Glass>[拨号音]

00:00:21.110 --> 00:00:26.460
<v Russian Soldier>[RUSSIAN SPEECH]
<v Russian Soldier>[俄语对话]

00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:36.120
<v Ira Glass>Ukraine's says over 3,500 soldiers have texted or called the number. One of my coworkers, Valerie Kipnis, has been looking into this hotline, and helpfully, speaks Russian. So what did they just say?
<v Ira Glass>乌克兰方面称，已有超过3500名士兵发送短信或拨打了该号码。我的同事瓦莱丽·基普尼斯一直在调查这条热线，幸运的是，她会说俄语。那么他们刚才说了什么？

00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:44.640
<v Valerie Kipnis>So they said, hello, to one another, and then the guy asked, is this Ukraine? And the guy on the hotline answered, yeah, this is Ukraine.
<v Valerie Kipnis>他们互相问候，然后那个人问，这是乌克兰吗？热线那边的人回答说，是的，这是乌克兰。

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:47.790
<v Russian Soldier>[RUSSIAN SPEECH]
<v Russian Soldier>[俄语对话]

00:00:47.790 --> 00:00:52.710
<v Valerie Kipnis>And then, the soldier said, I got a text that if we wish to surrender, call this number.
<v Valerie Kipnis>然后，那个士兵说，我收到一条短信，说如果我们想投降，就打这个号码。

00:00:52.710 --> 00:00:53.820
<v Ira Glass>He got a text?
<v Ira Glass>他收到了一条短信？

00:00:53.820 --> 00:01:00.880
<v Valerie Kipnis>Yeah, people pass around the number on social media and Telegram. There's even a website for it. It's called Hochy-Zhit dot com.
<v Valerie Kipnis>是的，人们在社交媒体和Telegram上转发这个号码。甚至还有一个专门的网站，叫“我想活下去”网站（Hochy-Zhit.com）。

00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:02.190
<v Ira Glass>And that means--
<v Ira Glass>那意思是——

00:01:02.190 --> 00:01:04.600
<v Valerie Kipnis>"I want to live" dot com.
<v Valerie Kipnis>“我想活下去”网站。

00:01:04.600 --> 00:01:06.300
<v Ira Glass>That's very direct.
<v Ira Glass>这非常直接。

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:25.470
<v Valerie Kipnis>Yeah. On it, it reads, "Servicemen of the Russian armed forces, do you notice that you're greeted not with flowers, but with fire and curses? Do you notice that your commanders run away first? Save your life, for yourself and your family. Stop fighting for other people's slogans. Contact us. Save your life."
<v Valerie Kipnis>是的。网站上写着：“俄罗斯武装部队的军人，你们注意到迎接你们的不是鲜花，而是炮火和诅咒吗？你们注意到你们的指挥官总是先逃跑吗？为了你自己和家人，拯救你的生命吧。别再为别人的口号而战了。联系我们。拯救你的生命。”

00:01:25.470 --> 00:01:27.810
<v Russian Soldier>[RUSSIAN SPEECH]
<v Russian Soldier>[俄语对话]

00:01:27.810 --> 00:01:29.250
<v Ira Glass>OK, so what happens in this call?
<v Ira Glass>好的，那通话里发生了什么？

00:01:29.250 --> 00:01:35.670
<v Valerie Kipnis>One interesting thing is that the guy doesn't seem to know if he can actually believe them. He's questioning if he can trust them.
<v Valerie Kipnis>有趣的一点是，那个人似乎不知道是否真的可以相信他们。他在质疑自己能否信任他们。

00:01:35.670 --> 00:01:37.410
<v Russian Soldier>[RUSSIAN SPEECH]
<v Russian Soldier>[俄语对话]

00:01:37.410 --> 00:01:40.130
<v Valerie Kipnis>He's saying, you won't be cutting our balls off?
<v Valerie Kipnis>他说，你们不会把我们的蛋蛋割掉吧？

00:01:40.130 --> 00:01:43.110
<v Hotline Worker>[RUSSIAN SPEECH]
<v Hotline Worker>[俄语对话]

00:01:43.110 --> 00:01:49.400
<v Valerie Kipnis>And then, the hotline guy answers, no, those are myths you guys are being fed. No one's going to be cutting anybody.
<v Valerie Kipnis>然后，热线那边的人回答说，不，那是你们被灌输的谣言。没有人会割掉谁的任何部位。

00:01:49.400 --> 00:01:49.860
<v Hotline Worker>[RUSSIAN SPEECH]
<v Hotline Worker>[俄语对话]

00:01:49.860 --> 00:01:50.760
<v Valerie Kipnis>Don't worry.
<v Valerie Kipnis>别担心。

00:01:50.760 --> 00:01:56.290
<v Ira Glass>Wait, but can I ask you, like, why would somebody who's calling this number-- like, why would they trust them?
<v Ira Glass>等等，但我能问一下吗，比如，为什么拨打这个号码的人——他们为什么要信任对方？

00:01:56.290 --> 00:02:17.430
<v Valerie Kipnis>Well, obviously, it's a scary decision, and they have to judge it for themselves. But on the website, it says, you know, we follow the Geneva Conventions for the treatment of prisoners of war. And they say they guarantee certain things-- three meals a day, medical service, legal support from international organizations, and regular communication with relatives.
<v Valerie Kipnis>嗯，显然这是一个可怕的决定，他们必须自己做出判断。但网站上说，我们遵守《日内瓦公约》中关于战俘待遇的规定。他们承诺保证一些事情——一日三餐、医疗服务、国际组织的法律援助，以及与亲属的定期联系。

00:02:17.430 --> 00:02:19.210
<v Ira Glass>What else happens in this call?
<v Ira Glass>通话中还发生了什么？

00:02:19.210 --> 00:02:23.790
<v Valerie Kipnis>Well, there's a bunch of stuff about, how does this actually work? Like, how do I surrender?
<v Valerie Kipnis>嗯，有很多关于“这到底是怎么运作的”内容？比如，我该如何投降？

00:02:23.790 --> 00:02:25.200
<v Russian Soldier>[RUSSIAN SPEECH]
<v Russian Soldier>[俄语对话]

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:27.630
<v Valerie Kipnis>Where should we come by? The soldier asks.
<v Valerie Kipnis>我们应该去哪里？士兵问。

00:02:27.630 --> 00:02:29.070
<v Hotline Worker>[RUSSIAN SPEECH]
<v Hotline Worker>[俄语对话]

00:02:29.070 --> 00:02:33.460
<v Valerie Kipnis>The hotline guy says, if you're fighting, then you're probably located in a certain region.
<v Valerie Kipnis>热线人员说，如果你在战斗，那么你可能就在某个特定区域。

00:02:33.460 --> 00:02:34.330
<v Russian Soldier>[RUSSIAN SPEECH]
<v Russian Soldier>[俄语对话]

00:02:34.330 --> 00:02:37.950
<v Valerie Kipnis>Well, yeah. And you'll come pick us up?
<v Valerie Kipnis>嗯，是的。你们会来接我们吗？

00:02:37.950 --> 00:02:38.850
<v Hotline Worker>[RUSSIAN SPEECH]
<v Hotline Worker>[俄语对话]

00:02:38.850 --> 00:02:47.190
<v Valerie Kipnis>Yeah. We need to understand where you are and whether you really want to surrender. For that, I need your information, and then I'll contact you.
<v Valerie Kipnis>是的。我们需要了解你在哪里，以及你是否真的想投降。为此，我需要你的信息，然后我会联系你。

00:02:47.190 --> 00:02:49.200
<v Hotline Worker>[RUSSIAN SPEECH]
<v Hotline Worker>[俄语对话]

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:51.210
<v Valerie Kipnis>And then, you can surrender, if you wish.
<v Valerie Kipnis>然后，如果你愿意，你就可以投降了。

00:02:51.210 --> 00:02:52.980
<v Ira Glass>Does the soldier sound nervous to you?
<v Ira Glass>你觉得那个士兵听起来紧张吗？

00:02:52.980 --> 00:02:58.170
<v Valerie Kipnis>Yeah. On the call, you can kind of hear him trying to make the decision of whether to do this or not.
<v Valerie Kipnis>是的。在通话中，你可以听到他似乎在努力做出是否要这样做的决定。

00:02:58.170 --> 00:03:00.760
<v Russian Soldier>[RUSSIAN SPEECH]
<v Russian Soldier>[俄语对话]

00:03:00.760 --> 00:03:14.670
<v Valerie Kipnis>We didn't even want to come here. We were ordered. Now, I'm thinking about what I can do. The guy in the hotline says, so, as I'm telling you, if you want to, you can surrender, and then return back home.
<v Valerie Kipnis>我们根本不想来这里。我们是奉命行事。现在，我在考虑我能做什么。热线的那个人说，正如我告诉你的，如果你想的话，你可以投降，然后回家。

00:03:14.670 --> 00:03:26.060
<v Valerie Kipnis>Please understand there's a Geneva convention that says that a combatant has no criminal liability, so long as he did not commit war crimes, in which case, you'll be returned home through a prisoner swap.
<v Valerie Kipnis>请理解，《日内瓦公约》规定，战斗人员只要没有犯下战争罪，就不承担刑事责任，在这种情况下，你会通过战俘交换被送回家。

00:03:26.060 --> 00:03:26.460
<v Russian Soldier>[RUSSIAN SPEECH]
<v Russian Soldier>[俄语对话]

00:03:26.460 --> 00:03:28.310
<v Valerie Kipnis>The soldier says, "Understood."
<v Valerie Kipnis>士兵说：“明白了。”

00:03:28.310 --> 00:03:28.680
<v Russian Soldier>[RUSSIAN SPEECH]
<v Russian Soldier>[俄语对话]

00:03:28.680 --> 00:03:29.890
<v Valerie Kipnis>Thank you.
<v Valerie Kipnis>谢谢。

00:03:29.890 --> 00:03:30.630
<v Hotline Worker>[RUSSIAN SPEECH]
<v Hotline Worker>[俄语对话]

00:03:30.630 --> 00:03:33.940
<v Valerie Kipnis>The hotline guy says, yeah, call any time.
<v Valerie Kipnis>热线的那个人说，好的，随时打电话。

00:03:33.940 --> 00:03:36.030
<v Ira Glass>OK, so then the guy just hung up?
<v Ira Glass>好的，然后那个人就挂断了？

00:03:36.030 --> 00:03:36.960
<v Valerie Kipnis>Yeah.
<v Valerie Kipnis>是的。

00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:39.210
<v Ira Glass>Do we have any idea, like, whether he went through with it?
<v Ira Glass>我们有办法知道他是否真的去投降了吗？

00:03:39.210 --> 00:03:40.610
<v Valerie Kipnis>No idea.
<v Valerie Kipnis>不知道。

00:03:40.610 --> 00:03:41.490
<v Ira Glass>Oh.
<v Ira Glass>噢。

00:03:41.490 --> 00:03:42.240
<v Valerie Kipnis>Yeah.
<v Valerie Kipnis>是的。

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:46.140
<v Ira Glass>Were you able to find anybody who actually used the hotline, and then surrendered?
<v Ira Glass>你能找到真正使用过这条热线并投降的人吗？

00:03:46.140 --> 00:03:54.570
<v Valerie Kipnis>So I spent a lot of time trying to find someone who had done that, and I stumbled across an interview with a guy who tried. And he told his whole story.
<v Valerie Kipnis>我花了很多时间试图寻找这样做过的人，我偶然发现了一次采访，受访者尝试过。他讲述了他的整个故事。

00:03:54.570 --> 00:03:57.150
<v Russian Soldier>[RUSSIAN SPEECH]
<v Russian Soldier>[俄语对话]

00:03:57.150 --> 00:03:58.590
<v Valerie Kipnis>So this is the guy.
<v Valerie Kipnis>所以这就是那个人。

00:03:58.590 --> 00:03:59.520
<v Ira Glass>Tell me about him.
<v Ira Glass>跟我说说他。

00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:06.870
<v Valerie Kipnis>He's young, 22 years old, kind of lanky. He was studying to be a computer programmer, and then was drafted into the army last fall.
<v Valerie Kipnis>他很年轻，22岁，人有点瘦长。他本来在学计算机编程，去年秋天被征召入伍。

00:04:06.870 --> 00:04:09.810
<v Ira Glass>Because they have, like, mandatory military service in Russia, still.
<v Ira Glass>因为在俄罗斯仍然有义务兵役制。

00:04:09.810 --> 00:04:16.380
<v Valerie Kipnis>Yeah. Every Russian guy has to do it. And there, he was assigned to be a rifleman and an assistant grenade launcher.
<v Valerie Kipnis>是的。每个俄罗斯男人都必须服役。在那里，他被分配为步枪手和副掷弹筒手。

00:04:16.380 --> 00:04:17.010
<v Ira Glass>Mm-hmm.
<v Ira Glass>嗯。

00:04:17.010 --> 00:04:22.740
<v Valerie Kipnis>But he had friends in Ukraine. And from the start of the invasion, he was very skeptical of the war.
<v Valerie Kipnis>但他有朋友在乌克兰。从入侵开始，他就对这场战争非常怀疑。

00:04:22.740 --> 00:04:24.610
<v Ira Glass>And is he part of the invasion?
<v Ira Glass>他参与了入侵吗？

00:04:24.610 --> 00:04:32.430
<v Valerie Kipnis>No, at least not right away. But he says he has a close friend who was sent into combat and dies the first day of the war.
<v Valerie Kipnis>没有，至少一开始没有。但他表示，他有一个亲密的朋友被派往战场，并在战争的第一天就阵亡了。

00:04:32.430 --> 00:04:33.270
<v Ira Glass>Oh, wow.
<v Ira Glass>天哪。

00:04:33.270 --> 00:04:44.790
<v Valerie Kipnis>Yeah. And then, he starts talking to his other friend, the only person he really trusts, who's in the army with him. And they start talking about, maybe we should surrender. Like, maybe we should leave.
<v Valerie Kipnis>是的。然后，他开始和他唯一真正信任的那个在部队里的朋友交谈。他们开始讨论，也许我们应该投降。也许我们应该离开。

00:04:44.790 --> 00:04:51.030
<v Valerie Kipnis>And at first, they're on the same page. But then, the closer they get to the front line, the less on-board his friend starts to get.
<v Valerie Kipnis>起初，他们想法一致。但随着他们离前线越来越近，他朋友的立场开始动摇了。

00:04:51.030 --> 00:04:52.160
<v Russian Soldier>[RUSSIAN SPEECH]
<v Russian Soldier>[俄语对话]

00:04:52.160 --> 00:04:54.630
<v Interviewer>[RUSSIAN SPEECH]
<v Interviewer>[俄语对话]

00:04:54.630 --> 00:05:10.810
<v Valerie Kipnis>And one night, after he gets news of a successful counteroffensive in Kharkiv, he decides to go alone. He's actually in Russia. That's where he's based. And he says, after an eight-hour shift, he walks out of the barracks, heads west with a map and compass, and walks for two days.
<v Valerie Kipnis>一天晚上，在他得知哈尔科夫反攻成功的消息后，他决定独自行动。他当时实际上在俄罗斯境内。他说，八小时轮班结束后，他走出兵营，拿着地图和指南针向西走，走了两天。

00:05:10.810 --> 00:05:12.930
<v Russian Soldier>[RUSSIAN SPEECH]
<v Russian Soldier>[俄语对话]

00:05:12.930 --> 00:05:13.890
<v Interviewer>[RUSSIAN SPEECH]
<v Interviewer>[俄语对话]

00:05:13.890 --> 00:05:18.780
<v Ira Glass>This seems so dangerous. Like, the Ukrainians could see him coming and just decide to shoot him.
<v Ira Glass>这看起来太危险了。乌克兰人可能会看到他过来，然后直接决定开枪射杀他。

00:05:18.780 --> 00:05:25.630
<v Valerie Kipnis>Yeah. Or the Russians could see him fleeing. And after walking, finally, he makes it to the border.
<v Valerie Kipnis>是的。或者俄罗斯人可能会看到他逃跑。在行走之后，他终于到达了边境。

00:05:25.630 --> 00:05:26.920
<v Russian Soldier>[RUSSIAN SPEECH]
<v Russian Soldier>[俄语对话]

00:05:26.920 --> 00:05:32.050
<v Valerie Kipnis>And that's when he calls the hotline. The interviewer asks him about it.
<v Valerie Kipnis>就在那时，他拨打了热线。采访者问了他相关的情况。

00:05:32.050 --> 00:05:33.940
<v Ira Glass>Wait, who is doing the interview?
<v Ira Glass>等等，是谁在采访？

00:05:33.940 --> 00:05:37.930
<v Valerie Kipnis>So it's one of these two Ukrainian YouTubers who seem to have some connection with the Ukrainian government.
<v Valerie Kipnis>是这两个乌克兰YouTuber中的一位，他们似乎与乌克兰政府有一些联系。

00:05:37.930 --> 00:05:42.700
<v Russian Soldier>[RUSSIAN SPEECH]
<v Russian Soldier>[俄语对话]

00:05:42.700 --> 00:05:52.710
<v Valerie Kipnis>The soldier says he heard about the hotline from friends living in Ukraine, and also, this girl, a good friend of his. The host asks, what's her name? She'll probably watch this.
<v Valerie Kipnis>那名士兵说，他是从住在乌克兰的朋友那里听说这条热线的，还有这个女孩，一个他的好朋友。主持人问，她叫什么名字？她可能会看这个视频。

00:05:52.710 --> 00:05:53.410
<v Russian Soldier>[RUSSIAN SPEECH]
<v Russian Soldier>[俄语对话]

00:05:53.410 --> 00:05:55.390
<v Valerie Kipnis>He says, her name is Anya.
<v Valerie Kipnis>他说，她叫安雅。

00:05:55.390 --> 00:05:59.150
<v Russian Soldier>[RUSSIAN SPEECH]
<v Russian Soldier>[俄语对话]

00:05:59.150 --> 00:06:10.350
<v Valerie Kipnis>He reaches the hotline over Telegram. They told him to make his way to a village in Ukraine, where Ukrainian servicemen would be waiting for him. He got his last bits of energy together and started to make his way over.
<v Valerie Kipnis>他通过Telegram联系上了热线。他们让他前往乌克兰的一个村庄，那里会有乌克兰士兵等着他。他集中了最后一点力气，开始向那边进发。

00:06:10.350 --> 00:06:12.370
<v Russian Soldier>[RUSSIAN SPEECH]
<v Russian Soldier>[俄语对话]

00:06:12.370 --> 00:06:17.110
<v Valerie Kipnis>When I got to where they told me to go, it was sunset, not totally dark yet.
<v Valerie Kipnis>当我到达他们告诉我的地方时，天色已晚，还没完全黑透。

00:06:17.110 --> 00:06:18.550
<v Russian Soldier>[RUSSIAN SPEECH]
<v Russian Soldier>[俄语对话]

00:06:18.550 --> 00:06:20.650
<v Valerie Kipnis>And I just looked at the village for a long time.
<v Valerie Kipnis>我看着那个村子看了很久。

00:06:20.650 --> 00:06:25.540
<v Russian Soldier>[RUSSIAN SPEECH]
<v Russian Soldier>[俄语对话]

00:06:25.540 --> 00:06:31.420
<v Valerie Kipnis>Truth be told, I was scared I wouldn't make it, that I'd just get shot. That was the scariest part.
<v Valerie Kipnis>老实说，我很害怕自己无法到达那里，害怕会被枪杀。那是最可怕的部分。

00:06:31.420 --> 00:06:33.520
<v Interviewer>[RUSSIAN SPEECH]
<v Interviewer>[俄语对话]

00:06:33.520 --> 00:06:45.430
<v Valerie Kipnis>And then, the interviewer interrupts. He says, seeing as you're here now, you didn't get shot. And he says, yeah, but I know what I look like in a Russian uniform. There could be an incident where I would get shot without any questions.
<v Valerie Kipnis>然后，采访者打断了他。他说，既然你现在在这儿，说明你没被枪杀。他说，是的，但我知道我穿着俄罗斯制服是什么样子。可能会发生我没问任何问题就被射杀的情况。

00:06:45.430 --> 00:06:46.930
<v Ira Glass>So what happens.
<v Ira Glass>那后来发生了什么？

00:06:46.930 --> 00:06:52.370
<v Valerie Kipnis>So once he gets there, he meets a Ukrainian woman and asks her for directions.
<v Valerie Kipnis>当他到达那里时，他遇到了一位乌克兰妇女，并向她问路。

00:06:52.370 --> 00:06:54.160
<v Russian Soldier>[RUSSIAN SPEECH]
<v Russian Soldier>[俄语对话]

00:06:54.160 --> 00:07:03.610
<v Valerie Kipnis>She says, my kids can walk you there. They're going to come back alive, right? And he swears that he won't hurt anyone. So the kids walk him to the school gymnasium.
<v Valerie Kipnis>她说，我的孩子可以带你去那里。他们能活着回来，对吧？他发誓他不会伤害任何人。于是孩子们带他去了学校体育馆。

00:07:03.610 --> 00:07:06.110
<v Valerie Kipnis>He gives the boy his pocketknife as a "thank you." And then, he's in--
<v Valerie Kipnis>他把折叠刀送给那个男孩作为“感谢”。然后，他就进去了——

00:07:06.110 --> 00:07:06.310
<v Russian Soldier>[RUSSIAN SPEECH]
<v Russian Soldier>[俄语对话]

00:07:06.310 --> 00:07:06.830
<v Interviewer>[RUSSIAN SPEECH]
<v Interviewer>[俄语对话]

00:07:06.830 --> 00:07:12.020
<v Valerie Kipnis>How many times were you beaten?
<v Valerie Kipnis>你被打了多少次？

00:07:12.020 --> 00:07:12.100
<v Russian Soldier>[RUSSIAN SPEECH]
<v Russian Soldier>[俄语对话]

00:07:12.100 --> 00:07:18.370
<v Valerie Kipnis>Not once.
<v Valerie Kipnis>一次都没有。

00:07:18.370 --> 00:07:19.000
<v Interviewer>[RUSSIAN SPEECH]
<v Interviewer>[俄语对话]

00:07:19.000 --> 00:07:19.570
<v Valerie Kipnis>How about tortured?
<v Valerie Kipnis>那被折磨呢？

00:07:19.570 --> 00:07:20.050
<v Russian Soldier>[RUSSIAN SPEECH]
<v Russian Soldier>[俄语对话]

00:07:20.050 --> 00:07:31.090
<v Valerie Kipnis>Never. In fact, they offered us candy and sweets. Though, I should point out, the UN has documented human rights abuses against Russian prisoners of war in Ukraine. They weren't reported to be systematic.
<v Valerie Kipnis>从来没有。事实上，他们还给了我们糖果和甜点。不过，我应该指出，联合国记录了乌克兰针对俄罗斯战俘的人权侵犯行为。据报道这些行为并非系统性的。

00:07:31.090 --> 00:07:37.820
<v Ira Glass>Yeah. Well, what about the fact that they're allowing video interviews with prisoners of war to go online?
<v Ira Glass>是的。那么，他们允许战俘的视频采访上网这件事呢？

00:07:37.820 --> 00:07:46.780
<v Valerie Kipnis>Yeah, that's potentially a violation of the Geneva Conventions. There are rules in there about not turning prisoners of war into subjects of public curiosity.
<v Valerie Kipnis>是的，这可能违反了《日内瓦公约》。公约中有规定，不得将战俘变成公众好奇的对象。

00:07:46.780 --> 00:07:51.900
<v Ira Glass>So then, after that, he's basically in a POW camp, like, waiting for a prisoner swap to go home?
<v Ira Glass>所以在那之后，他基本上就在战俘营里，等着通过战俘交换回家？

00:07:51.900 --> 00:08:10.340
<v Valerie Kipnis>I'm not sure he wants to go back. And I'm not sure what would happen to him if he did. He's given his full name on YouTube, admitting publicly he deserted the Russian army. Also, he says that he doesn't believe in Russia's version of the war. Like he says in the video, you're on your way to kill innocent people that are just defending their own country.
<v Valerie Kipnis>我不确定他想不想回去。我也不确定如果他回去了会发生什么。他在YouTube上给出了全名，公开承认自己从俄罗斯军队中开了小差。此外，他说他不相信俄罗斯版本的战争。就像他在视频里说的那样，你正要去杀害无辜的人，而这些人只是在保卫自己的国家。

00:08:10.340 --> 00:08:13.930
<v Interviewer>[RUSSIAN SPEECH]
<v Interviewer>[俄语对话]

00:08:13.930 --> 00:08:18.610
<v Valerie Kipnis>Then, when the interviewer asks him if he'd like to make a call home--
<v Valerie Kipnis>然后，当采访者问他是否想打个电话回家时——

00:08:18.610 --> 00:08:20.600
<v Russian Soldier>[RUSSIAN SPEECH]
<v Russian Soldier>[俄语对话]

00:08:20.600 --> 00:08:32.640
<v Valerie Kipnis>He says, no, politely. His parents have different views on the war than him. Instead, he calls his friend, Anya, the one who told him about the hotline. There's a short clip of him on the phone.
<v Valerie Kipnis>他礼貌地拒绝了。他父母对战争的看法与他不同。相反，他打电话给了那个告诉他热线的朋友安雅。有一小段他在通话的片段。

00:08:32.640 --> 00:08:35.950
<v Russian Soldier>[RUSSIAN SPEECH]
<v Russian Soldier>[俄语对话]

00:08:35.950 --> 00:08:38.240
<v Valerie Kipnis>He updates her on his status, and then says--
<v Valerie Kipnis>他向她更新了自己的近况，然后说——

00:08:38.240 --> 00:08:40.720
<v Russian Soldier>[RUSSIAN SPEECH]
<v Russian Soldier>[俄语对话]

00:08:40.720 --> 00:08:53.620
<v Valerie Kipnis>Tell me how you're doing. My life is such a mess right now.
<v Valerie Kipnis>告诉我你怎么样了。我的生活现在简直是一团糟。

00:08:53.620 --> 00:09:10.030
<v Ira Glass>The way Russia sees the war and the way Ukraine sees it are like two different realities. But you know, there are so many places where that kind of thing is true, where one reality bumps up against another. And people try to coax each other across the line from one side to the other.
<v Ira Glass>俄罗斯看待战争的方式和乌克兰看待战争的方式就像两个不同的现实。但你知道，有很多地方都有这种情况，即一个现实与另一个现实发生碰撞。人们试图诱导对方跨越界限，从一边走到另一边。

00:09:10.030 --> 00:09:43.030
<v Ira Glass>But crossing over-- not that many people do it, because it's so hard to do. It seems so dangerous. Today, on our program, we have people trying to sweet-talk each other across that line, with reason and with guile and with whatever they got. It's such an act of faith in each other even to try-- kind of inspiring, really. From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Stay with us, please.
<v Ira Glass>但跨越的人——并不多，因为这太难了。看起来太危险了。今天在我们的节目中，我们有人们试图用理智、诡计和他们所拥有的一切，来劝说对方跨越那道界限。仅仅是尝试这种互相信任的行为——确实有点鼓舞人心。来自芝加哥WBEZ，《美国生活》节目。我是艾拉·格拉斯。请继续收听。

00:09:43.030 --> 00:10:00.250
<v Ira Glass>Act 1, Flies, Meet Honey. So this woman, Aubree, has been calling elections administrators all over Texas for nearly two years. And as a case study of people trying to coax others over to their side, it's not going too well.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕，苍蝇与蜂蜜。这位叫奥布里的女士，两年来一直在给德克萨斯州各地的选举管理人员打电话。作为人们试图劝说他人站到自己一边的案例研究，进展并不太顺利。

00:10:00.250 --> 00:10:10.240
<v Ira Glass>She questions them, accuses them. There's one call I want to play a little bit of-- kind of, sums things up. At the beginning of the call, the official is very calm, saying things like--
<v Ira Glass>她质疑他们，指控他们。有一段通话我想播一小段——算是总结了一下。通话开始时，官员非常冷静，说着类似的话——

00:10:10.240 --> 00:10:14.200
<v Official>Aubree, you brought up a lot of things, so I wanted to tackle them one by one.
<v Official>奥布里，你提了很多问题，所以我想一个一个来解决。

00:10:14.200 --> 00:10:26.530
<v Ira Glass>But pretty quickly, Aubree is asking him about things that he can't comment on, because they're the subject of lawsuits. Lawyers have told them not to talk about them publicly. But Aubree-- she doesn't accept that. She keeps pushing.
<v Ira Glass>但很快，奥布里就开始问他一些他无法评论的事情，因为它们是诉讼的主题。律师告诉他们不要公开谈论这些事情。但奥布里——她不接受。她不断逼问。

00:10:26.530 --> 00:10:31.540
<v Aubree Campbell>Your county attorneys-- they don't want you to give information to the voters?
<v Aubree Campbell>你们的县检察官——他们不想让你向选民提供信息？

00:10:31.540 --> 00:10:38.470
<v Official>All I'm telling you is, I'm following their advice, Aubree. And I look forward to a time when I'm able to talk more freely about this.
<v Official>我所能告诉你的是，我正在遵循他们的建议，奥布里。我期待着有朝一日能更自由地谈论这个问题。

00:10:38.470 --> 00:10:44.160
<v Aubree Campbell>So you're not able to talk freely about the issues in the elections, because one part of it has been filed with a court of law?
<v Aubree Campbell>所以你不能自由地谈论选举中的问题，是因为其中一部分已经提交到了法院？

00:10:44.160 --> 00:10:46.870
<v Official>Aubree, did you not understand what I said earlier, when I said I'm not?
<v Official>奥布里，我刚才说的话你没听懂吗，我说我不能？

00:10:46.870 --> 00:10:48.290
<v Aubree Campbell>I do not. Could you help me understand--
<v Aubree Campbell>我没听懂。你能帮我理解一下吗——

00:10:48.290 --> 00:10:54.400
<v Official>I think I've spoken clearly and in English that I have, that I can't speak about this, because of the current and ongoing litigation.
<v Official>我想我已经用英语表达得很清楚了，由于目前正在进行的诉讼，我不能谈论这件事。

00:10:54.400 --> 00:11:08.560
<v Ira Glass>As you can hear, this gets hotter and hotter. He's an elections administrator in a county in central Texas and has had years of Stop the Stealers now, questioning him like this. They flood elections offices with records requests. They sue.
<v Ira Glass>正如你所听到的，火药味越来越浓。他是一位德克萨斯州中部的县选举管理员，现在已经忍受了多年的“停止窃选”抗议者这样质问他。他们用记录请求淹没选举办公室。他们起诉。

00:11:08.560 --> 00:11:23.920
<v Ira Glass>Before long in this call, Aubree is quoting Texas legal code that she believes mandates that all ballots in Texas should be pre-numbered sequentially, starting at one, which isn't done today, with computers counting votes. And the elections administrator snaps.
<v Ira Glass>通话不久后，奥布里引用了她认为要求德克萨斯州所有选票必须从第一号开始预先编号的德州法律，而现在使用计算机计票并不这么做。选举管理员爆发了。

00:11:23.920 --> 00:11:25.840
<v Official>What else would you like to quote, chapter and verse incorrectly?
<v Official>你还想引用哪一章哪一条的错误内容？

00:11:25.840 --> 00:11:30.820
<v Aubree Campbell>OK, sure. Sure. So you're sitting in violation of the state and national constitutions, because the--
<v Aubree Campbell>好的，当然。当然。所以你违反了州和国家宪法，因为——

00:11:30.820 --> 00:11:33.100
<v Official>Incorrect. Wrong. Next.
<v Official>不正确。错了。下一个。

00:11:33.100 --> 00:11:38.040
<v Aubree Campbell>Let me finish, sir. The Secretary of State does not have the authority to circumvent Texas election code, 52--
<v Aubree Campbell>让我说完，先生。国务卿没有权力绕过德克萨斯州选举法第52条——

00:11:38.040 --> 00:11:39.740
<v Official>You're mistaken. You're mistaken.
<v Official>你弄错了。你弄错了。

00:11:39.740 --> 00:11:40.240
<v Aubree Campbell>No, I'm not.
<v Aubree Campbell>不，我没有。

00:11:40.240 --> 00:11:45.190
<v Official>I don't even have to let you finish. I can tell you right now, off the bat, you're mistaken. Next.
<v Official>我甚至不需要让你说完。我现在就可以告诉你，一开始你就错了。下一个。

00:11:45.190 --> 00:11:51.670
<v Aubree Campbell>Can you tell me the election code that you can cite, that allows you to circumvent the 52.062 and 62.009?
<v Aubree Campbell>你能告诉我你引用的允许你绕过52.062和62.009的选举法吗？

00:11:51.670 --> 00:11:52.210
<v Official>Request denied.
<v Official>请求被拒绝。

00:11:52.210 --> 00:11:57.140
<v Aubree Campbell>You don't know it off the top of your head like I do? But you don't know the codes in the law off the top of your head?
<v Aubree Campbell>你记不住它吗？但你记不住法律条文吗？

00:11:57.140 --> 00:11:57.790
<v Official>I don't.
<v Official>我不记得。

00:11:57.790 --> 00:12:00.430
<v Aubree Campbell>OK. Do you-- have you read-- have you read the national--
<v Aubree Campbell>好的。你——你读过——你读过国家——

00:12:00.430 --> 00:12:04.330
<v Official>I'm pretty smart. Pretty smart. And yeah, I've read the Constitution.
<v Official>我很聪明。相当聪明。是的，我读过宪法。

00:12:04.330 --> 00:12:05.580
<v Official>[INTERPOSING VOICES]
<v Official>[交替的声音]

00:12:05.580 --> 00:12:10.420
<v Official>It's not as [INAUDIBLE] that. But you're not. You're not. What you're going to-- no, here's what you're going to do.
<v Official>它不像那样。但你不是。你不是。你要做什么——不，你要做的是。

00:12:10.420 --> 00:12:16.530
<v Aubree Campbell>No, no, no, you're not going to tell me what I'm going to do. You're going to review-- you're going to review the national and state constitutions, is what's going to happen.
<v Aubree Campbell>不，不，不，你不能告诉我我该做什么。你要审查——你要审查国家和州宪法，这就是要发生的事。

00:12:16.530 --> 00:12:18.610
<v Official>No, actually, it's not, Aubree--
<v Official>不，其实不是，奥布里——

00:12:18.610 --> 00:12:23.270
<v Aubree Campbell>Are you-- are you now refusing to review the national and state constitutions for your own--
<v Aubree Campbell>你是——你是现在拒绝审查国家和州宪法来为你的——

00:12:23.270 --> 00:12:35.690
<v Official>I am. And here's how I'm going to refuse that-- by the coming phone click that you're about to hear in 3, 2, 1.
<v Official>我是。这就是我拒绝的方式——通过你即将在3、2、1听到电话点击声。

00:12:35.690 --> 00:12:55.670
<v Ira Glass>Because of these kinds of encounters between elections officials and activists, it gets very personal sometimes. In the last few years in Texas, lots of election administrators have quit or retired. The entire staff of an elections office in Gillespie County quit at the same time, because of accusations of fraud, obscene, racist emails, threats.
<v Ira Glass>由于选举官员和活动人士之间发生此类冲突，有时会变得非常个人化。在过去几年的德克萨斯州，许多选举管理员辞职或退休。吉莱斯皮县选举办公室的全体工作人员同时辞职，原因就是欺诈指控、淫秽、种族主义邮件和威胁。

00:12:55.670 --> 00:13:12.880
<v Ira Glass>People call the cops on these officials and try to get them arrested while they're doing their jobs. But there is another path. That woman, Aubree Campbell, that you just heard-- she happens to live in a county in Texas-- Tarrant County, where the elections administrator is not the guy who you just heard on the phone.
<v Ira Glass>人们打电话给警察举报这些官员，并在他们工作时试图逮捕他们。但还有另一条路。你刚才听到的那个女人，奥布里·坎贝尔——她恰好住在德克萨斯州的一个县——塔兰特县，那里的选举管理员不是你刚才在电话里听到的那个人。

00:13:12.880 --> 00:13:31.900
<v Ira Glass>The official in Tarrant County is this singular, kind of, remarkable figure. We learned about him from Natalia Contreras, who's been reporting on elections in Texas for months now, for Votebeat, which covers all this in depth around the country. Natalia says, there's really nobody else like him that she's been able to find.
<v Ira Glass>塔兰特县的那位官员是一个独特且引人注目的形象。我们是从娜塔莉亚·孔特雷拉斯那里了解到的，她几个月来一直在为Votebeat报道德克萨斯州的选举，该网站深入报道了全国各地的情况。娜塔莉亚说，她找不到像他这样的人。

00:13:31.900 --> 00:13:50.980
<v Ira Glass>He's very effective, for some special reasons that Natalia will explain in a bit. His name is Heider Garcia. And watching him, it's hard not to feel like if anybody could convince the election doubters that our elections can be trusted-- or at the very least, if anybody can make headway with them, it would be this guy.
<v Ira Glass>他非常有效率，原因特殊，娜塔莉亚稍后会解释。他的名字叫海德·加西亚。看着他，很难不让人觉得，如果有人能说服那些质疑者相信我们的选举是可以信任的——或者至少，如果有人能在他们身上取得进展，那会是这个人。

00:13:50.980 --> 00:14:16.700
<v Ira Glass>And to try to see how successful he is in winning over the election skeptics and the Stop the Stealers, and to see the limits of that success. Earlier this month, on election day, Natalia and Zoe Chace from our staff co-reported a story where the two of them followed Heider around as he managed the election in Tarrant County and as he dealt with the election doubters-- and Aubree, in particular. Here are Natalia and Zoe.
<v Ira Glass>为了看看他在赢得选举怀疑论者和“停止窃选”派方面有多成功，并看看这种成功的局限性。本月早些时候，在选举日，娜塔莉亚和我们员工的佐伊·蔡斯合写了一篇报道，他们两人在海德管理塔兰特县选举并处理那些质疑者——特别是奥布里——时跟随他。这是娜塔莉亚和佐伊。

00:14:16.700 --> 00:14:25.620
<v Natalia Contreras>Let's start with Aubree. She's just one of many people in Texas who have made it almost their full-time job to challenge election officials.
<v Natalia Contreras>让我们从奥布里开始。她是德克萨斯州许多人中的一个，这些人几乎把挑战选举官员当作全职工作。

00:14:25.620 --> 00:14:40.440
<v Zoe Chace>And they all have some kind of story like this to explain how they got into it. In Tarrant County, when Joe Biden won, it didn't really make any sense to Aubree. It's a Republican county. Republicans won down the ballot. But the president-- somehow, Biden won.
<v Zoe Chace>他们都有一些这样的故事来解释他们是如何入行的。在塔兰特县，乔·拜登赢的时候，奥布里觉得这根本说不通。这是一个共和党县。共和党人在整个选票上都赢了。但总统——不知怎么的，拜登赢了。

00:14:40.440 --> 00:14:55.320
<v Aubree Campbell>Then, when Biden took Tarrant County, we were really upset, because Trump has a lot of vocal support here, and I don't know a single Democrat. And none of my Democrat friends voted for Biden in the primaries. They all voted for Bernie. So you talk to him one on one, they know something's not right.
<v Aubree Campbell>当拜登拿下塔兰特县时，我们真的很沮丧，因为特朗普在这里有很多支持者，我不认识一个民主党人。我的民主党朋友在初选中都没有投票给拜登。他们都投给了桑德斯。所以你一对一地和他们谈，他们知道有些事情是不对劲的。

00:14:55.320 --> 00:15:13.740
<v Natalia Contreras>Aubree doesn't look like other activists I see in this world, who are mostly elderly and retired. She's 33, but looks way younger. She's white, petite, round, hipstery eyeglasses, leather jacket. She describes herself as a conservative metal head. She stands out.
<v Natalia Contreras>奥布里看起来不像我在这个圈子里看到的其他大多是老年退休人员的活动人士。她33岁，看起来年轻得多。她是白人，娇小，圆脸，戴着时髦的眼镜，穿着皮夹克。她称自己为保守派金属乐迷。她很突出。

00:15:13.740 --> 00:15:20.610
<v Heider Garcia>It was post-2020, because I remember the whole thing started with her interacting with an election judge.
<v Heider Garcia>那是2020年之后，因为我记得整件事开始于她与一位选举裁判官的互动。

00:15:20.610 --> 00:15:22.050
<v Natalia Contreras>This is Heider Garcia.
<v Natalia Contreras>这是海德·加西亚。

00:15:22.050 --> 00:15:37.370
<v Heider Garcia>She was really frustrated. She took out her cell phone, started recording the poll worker, saying, you're obstructing me from voting. The poll worker said, you can't use your phone here, and the whole situation started to happen. And that's when I started interacting with her, and, you know.
<v Heider Garcia>她真的很沮丧。她拿出手机，开始录像那个工作人员，说，你在阻碍我投票。工作人员说，你不能在这里使用手机，整个局面就开始发生了。这就是我开始和她互动的时候。

00:15:37.370 --> 00:15:39.940
<v Heider Garcia>[RATTLING NOISE]
<v Heider Garcia>[咔嗒声]

00:15:39.940 --> 00:15:41.980
<v Heider Garcia>Coffee?
<v Heider Garcia>咖啡吗？

00:15:41.980 --> 00:15:58.250
<v Zoe Chace>It's 6:30 AM on election day. Heider Garcia is the kind of person who carries around his own coffee grinder to grind his own beans for his morning coffee. Fastidious? Neurotic? He has high standards for himself and his coffee.
<v Zoe Chace>选举日早上6:30。海德·加西亚就是那种随身携带咖啡研磨机自己磨咖啡豆的人。挑剔？神经质？他对自己的咖啡和自己都有很高的标准。

00:15:58.250 --> 00:16:11.510
<v Zoe Chace>He oversees every aspect of the election with the same fastidiousness. He sets the whole thing up. He trains the workers. He tells us he's been working on not being a micromanager, which you only say if you are.
<v Zoe Chace>他以同样的挑剔监督选举的每一个方面。他一手操办全局。他培训工作人员。他告诉我们他一直努力不做一个微观管理者，这只有当你是的时候才会这么说。

00:16:11.510 --> 00:16:31.220
<v Natalia Contreras>Heider's taking a particular approach with people like Aubree. It's something I haven't seen any other Texas election officials do. His strategy is to go as far as humanly possible to answer every question and address every suspicion. He treats everything they say like it's worthy of an answer.
<v Natalia Contreras>海德对像奥布里这样的人采取了一种特殊的方法。这是我没见过其他德克萨斯州选举官员做过的。他的策略是尽其所能回答每一个问题并解决每一个怀疑。他把他们说的每一件事都当作值得回答的事情来对待。

00:16:31.220 --> 00:16:44.330
<v Natalia Contreras>It's like radical patience. And when you see him do it, you see why other officials don't. Because it takes hours. It's a big part of his job now, on top of all the work of running an election.
<v Natalia Contreras>这就像极度的耐心。当你看到他这样做时，你就明白为什么其他官员不这么做了。因为它需要花费数小时。现在这是他工作的一大重点，此外还有运行选举的所有工作。

00:16:44.330 --> 00:16:51.170
<v Natalia Contreras>They call with some issue. He's like, come in. Let me walk you through it. Let me show you around. Here's a typical example.
