<v Zoe Chace>Does she sell merchandise?
<v Zoe Chace>她卖周边产品吗？

00:26:37.650 --> 00:26:39.670
<v Heider Garcia>Yep.
<v Heider Garcia>是的。

00:26:39.670 --> 00:26:41.380
<v Zoe Chace>What does she sell?
<v Zoe Chace>她卖什么？

00:26:41.380 --> 00:26:46.220
<v Heider Garcia>That stuff there. I bought two shirts and two mugs.
<v Heider Garcia>那些东西。我买了两个衬衫和两个杯子。

00:26:46.220 --> 00:26:48.110
<v Zoe Chace>No, you didn't.
<v Zoe Chace>不，你没有。

00:26:48.110 --> 00:26:58.900
<v Zoe Chace>He didn't. Aubree says she doesn't sell much merch, and she doesn't ask for donations much either. The merch reads "Taking Back Texas," which is Aubree's brand.
<v Zoe Chace>他没有。奥布里说她卖的不多，也不会要求太多捐款。周边商品上印着“收回德州”（Taking Back Texas），这是奥布里的品牌。

00:26:58.900 --> 00:27:08.240
<v Zoe Chace>Oh, that is a lot of merch.
<v Zoe Chace>哦，那可是很多周边。

00:27:08.240 --> 00:27:25.670
<v Natalia Contreras>Throughout the morning, Heider's constantly running back and forth, checking in on the people managing the phone bank, to the point where one of them literally says to him, Heider, I need to focus now. His phone keeps blowing up with calls about minor technical issues, machines going down at polling places.
<v Natalia Contreras>整个上午，海德不断跑来跑去，查看管理电话银行的人，以至于他们中的一个真的对他说了，海德，我现在需要专注。他的手机不断响着关于微小技术问题的电话，投票站的机器出了故障。

00:27:25.670 --> 00:27:33.230
<v Heider Garcia>Arlington, Miller-- that's the hot spot right now. I've got calls from both sides on that one.
<v Heider Garcia>阿灵顿，米勒——那是现在的热点。那件事我两边都接到了电话。

00:27:33.230 --> 00:27:49.310
<v Zoe Chace>There's other types of problems-- people problems. One poll worker, while demonstrating how to help voters vote, accidentally voted as someone else with a similar name. There's a problem with the poll judge-- well, with the alternate to the judge.
<v Zoe Chace>还有其他类型的问题——人员问题。一名投票站工作人员在演示如何帮助选民投票时，不小心以一个同名的人的身份投了票。选举裁判官有问题——好吧，是裁判官的替补。

00:27:49.310 --> 00:28:15.310
<v Heider Garcia>Question-- I do have one for you. Judge called and said she thinks her alternate has been drinking and is carrying-- [LAUGHS] --and carrying, and carrying. And apparently he said something like, I'm carrying, and I'm here to protect you if necessary. And she said she smelled alcohol. So we're going to tell him to call law enforcement and have them assess the situation.
<v Heider Garcia>问题——我确实有一个要问你。裁判官打电话说她认为她的替补喝了酒，并且带着——[笑] ——带着，带着。显然他说了一些类似的话，我带着武器，如果有必要我会保护你们。她说她闻到了酒精味。所以我们要让他打给执法部门，让他们评估情况。

00:28:15.310 --> 00:28:41.670
<v Zoe Chace>Mostly Heider's upbeat, excited, jokey. When his assistant Troy brags about how he's about to buy an F-150 hybrid, Heider's like, can you not drive it to work right after the election? People are going to be like, see, they got that big George Soros payout-- your "Ford Soros," he says.
<v Zoe Chace>海德大多很乐观、兴奋、爱开玩笑。当他的助手特洛伊吹嘘说他要买一辆福特F-150混动车时，海德说，你能在选举后别把它开到工作单位吗？人们会说，看，他们拿到了乔治·索罗斯那笔大钱——你的“福特索罗斯”，他说。

00:28:41.670 --> 00:28:46.190
<v Natalia Contreras>Heider's phone rings. It's Aubree again.
<v Natalia Contreras>海德的电话响了。又是奥布里。

00:28:46.190 --> 00:28:47.780
<v Heider Garcia>Hey.
<v Heider Garcia>嘿。

00:28:47.780 --> 00:28:59.930
<v Aubree Campbell>Hey, sorry. So someone kind of prominent locally is saying that she's been told machines in Hurst are down and that they're not going to be serviced or replaced.
<v Aubree Campbell>嘿，抱歉。所以当地有个相当知名的人说，她被告知赫斯特的机器坏了，而且不会被维修或更换。

00:28:59.930 --> 00:29:13.610
<v Natalia Contreras>In a mostly Republican part of town, a controller went out. The line was long. This is the kind of thing that happens in every election. Usually it's no big deal, but now everyone is suspicious they're being targeted.
<v Natalia Contreras>在城里一个主要是共和党人的地方，控制器坏了。排队很长。这是每次选举中都会发生的这类事情。通常这不是什么大事，但现在每个人都怀疑他们是被针对了。

00:29:13.610 --> 00:29:27.500
<v Heider Garcia>No, Brookside did have an issue. There was a tech dispatched there a while ago. They should be on-site, actually, right now. Yeah, nothing's not going to not get serviced. That'd be the stupidest mistake I can make. [LAUGHS]
<v Heider Garcia>不，布鲁克赛德确实出了问题。不久前有技术人员被派往那里。实际上，他们现在应该在现场了。是的，没有什么是不会被维修的。那是我能犯下的最愚蠢的错误。[笑]

00:29:27.500 --> 00:29:32.840
<v Aubree Campbell>All right, so do you have a lot of other locations where the controllers or the tabulator went down?
<v Aubree Campbell>好的，那你有其他控制器或计票器坏了的位置吗？

00:29:32.840 --> 00:29:49.490
<v Heider Garcia>I think a grapevine in the library-- they had two strings and one of them had an issue-- and Miller. I think that one was a scanner. But that's not a problem, because they can still vote. They just put them in the emergency bin on the board. Ballot board adds them in once we get it back here.
<v Heider Garcia>我想图书馆里有个葡萄园——他们有两串，其中一个出了问题——还有米勒。我想那个是扫描仪。但这没问题，因为他们仍然可以投票。他们只是把它们放进董事会的紧急箱里。计票委员会一旦把它们拿回来就会加入进去。

00:29:49.490 --> 00:29:53.540
<v Aubree Campbell>Oh, yeah, they're going to throw a fit about that though.
<v Aubree Campbell>哦，是的，不过他们会为此大发雷霆的。

00:29:53.540 --> 00:30:01.730
<v Natalia Contreras>This happened on a huge scale in Arizona, and people did throw a fit about it. Aubree tells Heider she's going to go check it out for herself.
<v Natalia Contreras>这种情况在亚利桑那州大规模发生，人们确实大发雷霆。奥布里告诉海德她要亲自去看看。

00:30:01.730 --> 00:30:06.890
<v Heider Garcia>I don't care if you call me 100 times a day, because I'd rather hear as soon as I can and get on it.
<v Heider Garcia>我不介意你一天打100次电话，因为我宁愿尽早听到并解决它。

00:30:06.890 --> 00:30:28.930
<v Zoe Chace>It's almost like he's deputized her. Sometimes they seem like colleagues. I can't tell whose strategy that is, or whether it's working, but it seems to calm things. In the end, the broken scanner wasn't a big deal.
<v Zoe Chace>简直就像他把她任命为副手一样。有时他们看起来像同事。我不知道那是谁的策略，或者是否有效，但这似乎让事情平静了下来。最后，扫描仪坏掉并不是什么大事。

00:30:28.930 --> 00:30:41.950
<v Natalia Contreras>Finally, when we get near 7:00 when polls will close, poll watchers start piling in from voting sites around the county. Not as many as we had expected. When Aubree walks in, her first question is about Heider.
<v Natalia Contreras>最后，当接近7:00投票站即将关闭时，观察员开始从全县的投票点涌入。没有我们预期的那么多。当奥布里走进来时，她的第一个问题是关于海德的。

00:30:41.950 --> 00:30:57.610
<v Natalia Contreras>"Do you know where he is?" she says to someone. "He probably doesn't want me walking around here too much on my own." Again, Heider and Aubree interact like colleagues almost-- colleagues who don't really like each other, frenemies?
<v Natalia Contreras>“你知道他在哪吗？”她对某人说。“他可能不想让我自己在这里走来走去。”又是这样，海德和奥布里之间的互动几乎就像同事——不太喜欢对方的同事，冤家？

00:30:57.610 --> 00:30:58.420
<v Heider Garcia>Hello?
<v Heider Garcia>喂？

00:30:58.420 --> 00:30:59.180
<v Aubree Campbell>Hey, how are you?
<v Aubree Campbell>嘿，你好吗？

00:30:59.180 --> 00:31:00.280
<v Heider Garcia>I'm doing good. How's it going?
<v Heider Garcia>我很好。进展如何？

00:31:00.280 --> 00:31:01.000
<v Aubree Campbell>Good.
<v Aubree Campbell>很好。

00:31:01.000 --> 00:31:05.110
<v Natalia Contreras>Notice how they're talking past each other-- but so politely.
<v Natalia Contreras>注意他们是如何客气地自说自话的。

00:31:05.110 --> 00:31:07.280
<v Aubree Campbell>The lines were kind of long, but--
<v Aubree Campbell>队伍有点长，但是——

00:31:07.280 --> 00:31:09.550
<v Heider Garcia>But they're moving quick, aren't they?
<v Heider Garcia>但移动得很快，不是吗？

00:31:09.550 --> 00:31:14.050
<v Aubree Campbell>Well, the ones I went to, what's it called, Euless Family Center?
<v Aubree Campbell>嗯，我去的那些，叫什么，尤利斯家庭中心？

00:31:14.050 --> 00:31:14.680
<v Heider Garcia>Uh-huh.
<v Heider Garcia>嗯哼。

00:31:14.680 --> 00:31:16.330
<v Aubree Campbell>Oh, my gosh.
<v Aubree Campbell>天哪。

00:31:16.330 --> 00:31:17.860
<v Heider Garcia>That's a great place. It's really big, open.
<v Heider Garcia>那是个好地方。它很大，开放。

00:31:17.860 --> 00:31:18.550
<v Aubree Campbell>It is.
<v Aubree Campbell>是的。

00:31:18.550 --> 00:31:19.450
<v Heider Garcia>Is that the one? Yeah.
<v Heider Garcia>是那个吗？是的。

00:31:19.450 --> 00:31:21.770
<v Aubree Campbell>But, man, they had so many people.
<v Aubree Campbell>但是，天哪，人太多了。

00:31:21.770 --> 00:31:22.810
<v Heider Garcia>Yeah.
<v Heider Garcia>是的。

00:31:22.810 --> 00:31:26.180
<v Aubree Campbell>And they said they could have used an extra poll book or two.
<v Aubree Campbell>他们说他们本来可以用额外的投票登记册。

00:31:26.180 --> 00:31:29.520
<v Heider Garcia>OK. This way. Well, you know the way.
<v Heider Garcia>好的。这边。好吧，你知道路。

00:31:29.520 --> 00:31:30.760
<v Aubree Campbell>I know.
<v Aubree Campbell>我知道。

00:31:30.760 --> 00:31:35.500
<v Zoe Chace>Finally, it's time to count the votes. There's one hilarious looking ritual first.
<v Zoe Chace>终于到了计票的时候了。首先有一个看起来很搞笑的仪式。

00:31:35.500 --> 00:31:37.470
<v Heider Garcia>Oh, here we go!
<v Heider Garcia>哦，开始了！

00:31:37.470 --> 00:31:39.040
<v Aubree Campbell>Oh.
<v Aubree Campbell>哦。

00:31:39.040 --> 00:31:41.080
<v Heider Garcia>The walk of shame.
<v Heider Garcia>羞耻之行。

00:31:41.080 --> 00:32:00.380
<v Zoe Chace>The absentee ballots are on a USB drive. And to move that USB drive into the tally room where they'll be added to the results, the drive is lifted high into the air for all watchers to see and marched like a winning quarterback into the next room. It's symbolic of Heider's whole approach to things-- flamboyantly transparent.
<v Zoe Chace>缺席选票在USB驱动器上。为了把那个驱动器带进计票室加入结果，驱动器被高高举起给所有观察员看，像获胜的四分卫一样行进到下一个房间。这象征着海德处理事情的整个方式——华丽地透明。

00:32:00.380 --> 00:32:02.080
<v Heider Garcia>No, that's why we have the GoPro.
<v Heider Garcia>不，这就是我们有GoPro的原因。

00:32:02.080 --> 00:32:10.870
<v Zoe Chace>Someone holds a GoPro up high so the entire march of the USB drive is recorded in case anyone asks later, what happened between the two rooms?
<v Zoe Chace>有人把GoPro高高举起，这样USB驱动器的整个行进过程都被记录下来，以防有人后来问，两个房间之间发生了什么？

00:32:10.870 --> 00:32:23.350
<v Heider Garcia>We should do like a chair, like a throne chair, and we should carry it on the shoulders, all of us in the procession, but with just a USB drive sitting on the chair.
<v Heider Garcia>我们应该弄把椅子，像宝座一样，我们应该把它扛在肩上，我们所有人都在队列中，但上面只放一个USB驱动器。

00:32:23.350 --> 00:32:30.130
<v Natalia Contreras>We get to the back room where they're counting votes. This is where the hardcore election skeptics are camped out for the night.
<v Natalia Contreras>我们到了他们计票的后室。这里是那些死硬的选举怀疑论者彻夜驻扎的地方。

00:32:30.130 --> 00:32:49.570
<v Natalia Contreras>There's Amy. She's in a red, white, and blue dress with a clipboard, scrutinizing the way the ballot bags are sealed. Mike, he's a retired air mechanic who says, if we can audit planes, why can't we do better with elections here? He's there. Karen, she has a lawsuit against Heider.
<v Natalia Contreras>那是艾米。她穿着一件红白蓝相间的裙子，拿着一块写字板，仔细检查选票袋的密封方式。迈克，他是一位退休的飞机机械师，他说，如果我们能审计飞机，为什么选举不能做得更好？他在那儿。凯伦，她对海德有诉讼。

00:32:49.570 --> 00:33:02.260
<v Natalia Contreras>There's maybe a dozen people he deals with regularly, like Aubree. He knows all of these people well, and he's trying to anticipate what they'll want. And does it work? Are they satisfied?
<v Natalia Contreras>可能有十几个人是他经常处理的，比如奥布里。他非常了解这些人，他试图预测他们想要什么。这有效吗？他们满意吗？

00:33:02.260 --> 00:33:08.290
<v Aubree Campbell>I will say this, Heider makes other election administrators look like idiots.
<v Aubree Campbell>我会说这个，海德让其他选举管理员看起来像白痴。

00:33:08.290 --> 00:33:09.700
<v Natalia Contreras>Again, here's Aubree.
<v Natalia Contreras>再说一次，这是奥布里。

00:33:09.700 --> 00:33:15.940
<v Aubree Campbell>Well, he'll answer all your questions, so we're getting places. He's made some changes that I like.
<v Aubree Campbell>嗯，他会回答你所有的问题，所以我们正在取得进展。他做了一些我喜欢的改变。

00:33:15.940 --> 00:33:17.080
<v Natalia Contreras>Like what?
<v Natalia Contreras>比如什么？

00:33:17.080 --> 00:33:28.060
<v Aubree Campbell>He's adding all of the result tapes for election day onto the website. He's doing a lot of stuff that's helping, and that's more than other election administrators.
<v Aubree Campbell>他把选举日的所有结果胶带都加到了网站上。他做了很多有帮助的事情，这比其他选举管理员要多。

00:33:28.060 --> 00:33:36.580
<v Natalia Contreras>Do you think that's helped in any way your confidence in the system, if you will?
<v Natalia Contreras>你认为这对你对系统的信心有帮助吗？

00:33:36.580 --> 00:33:42.340
<v Aubree Campbell>No, not in the system. I do not trust the system. But it's helped me understand the system more.
<v Aubree Campbell>不，不是对系统的信心。我不信任这个系统。但这帮助我更多地了解了系统。

00:33:42.340 --> 00:33:54.590
<v Aubree Campbell>Trust it? No. I don't trust computerized voting systems. Computers can be hacked-- they always can. Whether that's happening right now, we're unable to fully investigate.
<v Aubree Campbell>信任它？不。我不信任计算机化的投票系统。电脑可能会被黑客攻击——它们总是可以的。无论现在是否正在发生，我们无法完全调查。

00:33:54.590 --> 00:34:10.310
<v Zoe Chace>Even after everything Heider's done to convince her otherwise, she doesn't believe the process is secure enough in Tarrant County to say for sure that the votes were accurately counted. This past year I've wondered, can you convince someone that an election is not stolen?
<v Zoe Chace>即使在海德做了所有这一切来说服她之后，她仍然不相信塔兰特县的过程足够安全，以至于不能确定选票是被准确计算的。过去一年我一直在想，你能说服某人选举没有被偷走吗？

00:34:10.310 --> 00:34:29.780
<v Zoe Chace>And what I realized watching Heider is, that is not the right question to ask right now. That's an endpoint we're not going to arrive at. The activists aren't going away. Instead, what Heider is trying, he's integrating the election skeptics and all their questions into the elections office and the way it works.
<v Zoe Chace>我看着海德意识到，现在问这个问题是不对的。那是我们无法到达的终点。活动人士不会离开。相反，海德正在尝试的，是将选举怀疑论者及其所有问题整合到选举办公室及其运作方式中。

00:34:29.780 --> 00:34:38.390
<v Heider Garcia>It's the job. Maybe there's no point. Maybe that's just the job. You know, maybe there's no endgame. Maybe that's just the way it is.
<v Heider Garcia>这就是工作。也许没意义。也许这就是工作。你知道，也许没有终局。也许事情就是这样。

00:34:38.390 --> 00:34:51.030
<v Heider Garcia>Like I said, if there is a shooting, police jumps into it. If there was a fire, a firefighter. [INAUDIBLE] questions, that's ours. Not because it's going to end, because it's your job. It's your job, it's simple.
<v Heider Garcia>就像我说的，如果发生枪击，警察就会介入。如果发生火灾，消防员就会介入。[听不清] 问题，那是我们的。不是因为它会结束，因为这是你的工作。这是你的工作，很简单。

00:34:51.030 --> 00:35:04.920
<v Natalia Contreras>And in this new normal on this election night, the job's gone as well as he could hope. The activists still have their doubts, a few of them already setting up meetings with Heider for later that week. There's a new conspiracy every day.
<v Natalia Contreras>在这个选举之夜的新常态下，工作进行得和他希望的一样顺利。活动人士仍然有疑问，其中几个人已经安排好那周晚些时候和海德见面。每天都有一个新的阴谋。

00:35:04.920 --> 00:35:29.590
<v Natalia Contreras>But no one yells. No one throws a fit saying they're being obstructed. And Heider rushes around to everyone, like the anxious owner of a restaurant, checking on their regulars, making sure they have everything they need. And the votes get counted.
<v Natalia Contreras>但没有人尖叫。没有人因为说他们被阻碍而大发雷霆。海德像个焦虑的餐馆老板一样围着每个人跑，查看他们的常客，确保他们拥有所需的一切。选票也被计算了。

00:35:29.590 --> 00:35:38.870
<v Ira Glass>Natalia Contreras is a reporter with Votebeat. You can find her work at Votebeat.org. Zoe Chace is a producer on our show.
<v Ira Glass>娜塔莉亚·孔特雷拉斯是Votebeat的记者。你可以在Votebeat.org找到她的作品。佐伊·蔡斯是我们节目的制作人。

00:35:38.870 --> 00:35:55.920
<v Ira Glass>Coming up, we watch a guy during the exact moment he is crossing through the looking glass to the other side. And we can see it, because he's on national TV on a game show. That's in a minute on Chicago Public Radio when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>接下来，我们将看到一个人在穿过镜子走到另一边的那一刻。我们可以看到，因为他在电视节目上。那是芝加哥公共广播电台一分钟后的节目。

00:35:55.920 --> 00:36:06.020
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Today on our program, Through the Looking Glass. People trying to coax each other across the border from one way of seeing the world to another way.
<v Ira Glass>这是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。今天在我们的节目中，透过镜子。人们试图诱导对方跨越界限，从一种看待世界的方式到另一种方式。

00:36:06.020 --> 00:36:12.000
<v Ira Glass>We have arrived at Act Two of our show. Act Two, Who is Ryan Long?
<v Ira Glass>我们已经到了我们节目的第二幕。第二幕，谁是瑞安·朗？

00:36:12.000 --> 00:36:24.950
<v Ira Glass>So in this story you're going to see a guy trying to actually coax himself through the looking glass from one reality into another. Lots of people actually saw him do this. One of our producers, Bim Adewunmi, was one of them.
<v Ira Glass>在这个故事中，你会看到一个人试图诱导自己穿过镜子，从一个现实进入另一个现实。很多人亲眼目睹他这样做。我们的制作人之一，比姆·阿德温米，就是其中之一。

00:36:24.950 --> 00:36:36.290
<v Bim Adewunmi>Back in May, I became mildly obsessed with a stranger on TV. It was a big guy. He looked Black, biracial maybe, and he was wearing a light purple shirt under a black vest.
<v Bim Adewunmi>回到五月，我开始有点痴迷于电视上的一个陌生人。那是个大块头。他看起来是黑人，也许是混血儿，他穿了一件浅紫色衬衫，外面穿了一件黑色背心。

00:36:36.290 --> 00:36:42.080
<v Bim Adewunmi>I was ill on my sofa. It was 7:00 PM on a Friday. You know what that means.
<v Bim Adewunmi>我当时生病躺在沙发上。那是周五晚上7:00。你知道那意味着什么。

00:36:42.080 --> 00:36:54.270
<v Johnny Gilbert>This is Jeopardy! Introducing today's contestants, a rideshare driver from Philadelphia, Pennsylvania, Ryan Long.
<v Johnny Gilbert>这是《危险边缘》（Jeopardy!）。介绍今天的选手，来自宾夕法尼亚州费城的拼车司机，瑞安·朗。

00:36:54.270 --> 00:37:11.760
<v Bim Adewunmi>A rideshare driver. I watch a lot of Jeopardy, but if you don't, you might not know that it is not that common among all the professors and the grad students and other learned contestants to see an Uber driver. Also unusual in my years of watching-- big Black guys.
<v Bim Adewunmi>拼车司机。我看很多《危险边缘》，但如果你不看，你可能不知道在所有教授、研究生和其他博学的选手中间，看到一个优步司机并不常见。在我看节目的这些年里，这也是不寻常的——高大的黑人。

00:37:11.760 --> 00:37:40.710
<v Bim Adewunmi>Ryan won that first game, and then he kept on winning. In the end, he won almost $300,000 over 16 games, becoming the ninth winningest Jeopardy winner of all time. After pretty much every win, he would do this thing that I began to look forward to. He'd grip the lectern, look down, almost defiantly refusing to meet the host's eye, and shake his head. His body language basically screamed "this can't be real, this is impossible."
<v Bim Adewunmi>瑞安赢得了第一场比赛，然后他一直赢。最终，他在16场比赛中赢得了近30万美元，成为《危险边缘》历史上第九大赢家。几乎每次获胜后，他都会做一个我开始期待的动作。他会抓住讲台，低头看，几乎是挑衅地拒绝直视主持人的眼睛，然后摇头。他的肢体语言基本上尖叫着“这不可能是真的，这不可能”。

00:37:40.710 --> 00:37:46.890
<v Mayim Bialik>Ryan, congratulations! With $18,800, you are our new Jeopardy champion.
<v Mayim Bialik>瑞安，祝贺你！以18,800美元，你是我们新的《危险边缘》冠军。

00:37:46.890 --> 00:37:57.900
<v Bim Adewunmi>I took a photo of Ryan on my TV screen that first night he won, and posted it to Instagram. "You have my sword," I captioned the photo, a line from "The Lord of the Rings."
<v Bim Adewunmi>在那晚他获胜后，我给电视屏幕上的瑞安拍了张照片，并发布到Instagram上。“你拥有我的剑，”我为这张照片配文，这是《指环王》中的一句台词。

00:37:57.900 --> 00:38:11.910
<v Bim Adewunmi>Here's a thing about me-- I love trivia. I was a childhood library lurker who hoarded facts, just waiting to bust them out and impress people. I love remembering dates and locations, and I delight in wordplay.
<v Bim Adewunmi>关于我有一件事——我热爱冷知识。我是一个儿时的图书馆徘徊者，囤积事实，只等着把它们爆出来给人留下深刻印象。我喜欢记住日期和地点，我喜欢文字游戏。

00:38:11.910 --> 00:38:26.910
<v Bim Adewunmi>Watching Ryan win again and again reminded me of myself a little, except he was me to the power of 100. It sounds condescending and weird, because we're about the same age, in our very late 30s, but I was proud of him, I think.
<v Bim Adewunmi>看着瑞安一次又一次获胜，让我想起了一点点我自己，除了他是我的100倍。这听起来有点居高临下和奇怪，因为我们年纪相仿，在30多岁末尾，但我为他感到骄傲，我想。

00:38:26.910 --> 00:38:37.050
<v Bim Adewunmi>I rarely feel affection for Jeopardy winners. They're too mannered, too serious, too odd. But I was rooting for Ryan. I felt like I knew him.
<v Bim Adewunmi>我很少对《危险边缘》的赢家感到亲切。他们太讲究、太严肃、太古怪。但我支持瑞安。我觉得我认识他。

00:38:37.050 --> 00:38:58.810
<v Bim Adewunmi>He had tweeted after his winning run something that stuck with me, something about a box. "Sometimes," he wrote, "it seems like society put you in a box and you are classified as a certain thing, with a certain destiny, even though you may feel differently inside."
<v Bim Adewunmi>他在获胜后发的一条推文引起了我的共鸣，关于一个盒子的事情。“有时，”他写道，“社会似乎把你放在一个盒子里，你被归类为某种东西，有着某种命运，尽管你在内心深处可能有不同的感觉。”

00:38:58.810 --> 00:39:11.560
<v Bim Adewunmi>I wondered how somebody who felt so familiar to me had ended up dominating the Jeopardy stage. What was that like for him? What box did he think he was in, and how had Jeopardy helped him break out of it?
<v Bim Adewunmi>我想知道一个对我感觉如此熟悉的人是怎么最终统治了《危险边缘》舞台的。对他来说那是什么感觉？他认为自己在什么盒子里，以及《危险边缘》是如何帮助他打破它的？

00:39:11.560 --> 00:39:22.680
<v Bim Adewunmi>Honestly, I just wanted to meet him. And so, along with my colleague and co-Ryan fan, Zoe Chace, I got on a train to Philadelphia. He met us in his new truck at the station.
<v Bim Adewunmi>老实说，我只是想见他。于是，我和我的同事，瑞安的忠实粉丝，佐伊·蔡斯，坐火车去了费城。他在车站接到了我们，开着他的新卡车。

00:39:22.680 --> 00:39:22.990
<v Zoe Chace>Hi, Ryan.
<v Zoe Chace>嘿，瑞安。

00:39:22.990 --> 00:39:51.340
<v Bim Adewunmi>Which, by the way, we knew immediately that was Ryan's car. It still has that new car gleam, and it is huge-- exactly the car you would expect from a man whose happy place is his car. Even before he became a rideshare driver, he would spend hours driving around navigating the potholes of Philly with his music on. So Zoe and I ended up spending all day on the road with him and meeting a couple of his friends. And the first question from Ryan felt like something you might ask a friend.
<v Bim Adewunmi>顺便说一句，我们立刻就知道那是瑞安的车。它仍然有那种新车的光泽，而且它很大——正是你从一个以车为乐的人那里所期望的车。甚至在他成为拼车司机之前，他就会花几个小时开车在费城的坑洼中导航，播放着音乐。所以佐伊和我最后整天和他在一起，并见了他的几个朋友。瑞安的第一个问题听起来像是你可以问朋友的问题。

00:39:51.340 --> 00:39:56.260
<v Ryan Long>All right, let's start off with a weird question. Do either one of you have any lotion?
<v Ryan Long>好了，让我们从一个奇怪的问题开始。你们俩有人带乳液吗？

00:39:56.260 --> 00:39:57.040
<v Zoe Chace>Lotion?
<v Zoe Chace>乳液？

00:39:57.040 --> 00:39:58.810
<v Bim Adewunmi>I have hand cream, is that going to do it?
<v Bim Adewunmi>我有护手霜，那个可以吗？

00:39:58.810 --> 00:39:59.500
<v Ryan Long>That'll do.
<v Ryan Long>那个就行。

00:39:59.500 --> 00:40:00.040
<v Bim Adewunmi>OK.
<v Bim Adewunmi>好的。

00:40:00.040 --> 00:40:02.000
<v Zoe Chace>I just have face moisturizer.
<v Zoe Chace>我只有面部保湿霜。

00:40:02.000 --> 00:40:03.520
<v Ryan Long>Yeah, my arms are so dry.
<v Ryan Long>是的，我的胳膊太干了。

00:40:03.520 --> 00:40:04.810
<v Bim Adewunmi>Oh, bless you.
<v Bim Adewunmi>哦，保佑你。

00:40:04.810 --> 00:40:12.760
<v Ira Glass>And about 20 minutes after we'd met, an aside about Michael Jackson's autobiography somehow led us here.
<v Ira Glass>大约在我们见面20分钟后，关于迈克尔·杰克逊自传的一个侧面评论不知何故把我们带到了这里。

00:40:12.760 --> 00:40:14.380
<v Bim Adewunmi>Who's your favorite Bond?
<v Bim Adewunmi>你最喜欢的邦德是谁？

00:40:14.380 --> 00:40:15.150
<v Ryan Long>Connery, what?
<v Ryan Long>康纳利，什么？

00:40:15.150 --> 00:40:15.570
<v Bim Adewunmi>Wow.
<v Bim Adewunmi>哇。

00:40:15.570 --> 00:40:17.620
<v Ryan Long>What?
<v Ryan Long>什么？

00:40:17.620 --> 00:40:24.820
<v Bim Adewunmi>That's amazing. I feel like Connery is up there, but you know who my favorite is, seriously?
<v Bim Adewunmi>那太棒了。我觉得康纳利很棒，但你知道我最喜欢的是谁吗，说真的？

00:40:24.820 --> 00:40:26.320
<v Ryan Long>Don't say Daniel Craig.
<v Ryan Long>别说是丹尼尔·克雷格。

00:40:26.320 --> 00:40:26.870
<v Bim Adewunmi>No.
<v Bim Adewunmi>不。

00:40:26.870 --> 00:40:28.000
<v Ryan Long>Oh. Timothy Dalton?
<v Ryan Long>哦。蒂莫西·道尔顿？

00:40:28.000 --> 00:40:32.140
<v Bim Adewunmi>Timothy Dalton!
<v Bim Adewunmi>蒂莫西·道尔顿！

00:40:32.140 --> 00:40:33.520
<v Ryan Long>He never got the respect he deserved.
<v Ryan Long>他从来没有得到他应得的尊重。

00:40:33.520 --> 00:40:34.210
<v Bim Adewunmi>He never got--
<v Bim Adewunmi>他从来没有得到——

00:40:34.210 --> 00:40:34.600
<v Ryan Long>Never!
<v Ryan Long>从来没有！

00:40:34.600 --> 00:40:35.880
<v Bim Adewunmi>--the respect he deserved!
<v Bim Adewunmi>——他应得的尊重！

00:40:35.880 --> 00:40:36.310
<v Ryan Long>No.
<v Ryan Long>不。

00:40:36.310 --> 00:40:37.090
<v Bim Adewunmi>OK, good.
<v Bim Adewunmi>好的，好极了。

00:40:37.090 --> 00:40:45.310
<v Ira Glass>I'll tell you this for free-- There is no loneliness like the loneliness of those of us in the "Timothy Dalton is the best James Bond" camp.
<v Ira Glass>我免费告诉你这个——没有一种孤独像我们这些“蒂莫西·道尔顿是最好的詹姆斯·邦德”阵营里的那种孤独。

00:40:45.310 --> 00:40:45.970
<v Ryan Long>I'm telling you.
<v Ryan Long>我告诉你。

00:40:45.970 --> 00:40:46.960
<v Bim Adewunmi>Oh, my god. I love Timothy Dalton so much.
<v Bim Adewunmi>噢，天哪。我太爱蒂莫西·道尔顿了。

00:40:46.960 --> 00:40:53.390
<v Ryan Long>I watched "The Living Daylights" so many times. And the way he always hit the words.
<v Ryan Long>我看过《黎明杀机》那么多次。他说话的方式。

00:40:53.390 --> 00:40:53.710
<v Bim Adewunmi>Yes!
<v Bim Adewunmi>是的！

00:40:53.710 --> 00:40:56.780
<v Ryan Long>Yes! Talking to the-- what's the Russian guy? Georgi--
<v Ryan Long>是的！跟那个——那个俄罗斯人叫什么？乔治——

00:40:56.780 --> 00:40:57.250
<v Bim Adewunmi>Yeah.
<v Bim Adewunmi>是的。

00:40:57.250 --> 00:40:57.790
<v Ryan Long>--Koskov.
<v Ryan Long>——科斯科夫。

00:40:57.790 --> 00:40:58.210
<v Bim Adewunmi>Koskov!
<v Bim Adewunmi>科斯科夫！

00:40:58.210 --> 00:40:58.450
<v Ryan Long>Koskov!
<v Ryan Long>科斯科夫！

00:40:58.450 --> 00:40:58.890
<v Bim Adewunmi>Koskov!
<v Bim Adewunmi>科斯科夫！

00:40:58.890 --> 00:40:59.770
<v Bim Adewunmi>[LAUGHS]
<v Bim Adewunmi>[笑]

00:40:59.770 --> 00:41:05.170
<v Bim Adewunmi>And when he's talking, he goes, "Felix Leiter," you could tell he was a stage actor.
<v Bim Adewunmi>当他说话时，他说，“费利克斯·雷特”，你可以看出他是位舞台演员。

00:41:05.170 --> 00:41:18.580
<v Ira Glass>The road to the Alex Trebek stage at Sony Pictures Studios started early for Ryan. Like me, he was a voracious reader who liked to read the dictionary for fun. Unlike me, Ryan was reading the newspaper by the age of two.
<v Ira Glass>通往索尼影业摄影棚的亚历克斯·特雷贝克舞台的道路对于瑞安来说开始得很早。和我一样，他是个贪婪的读者，喜欢为了娱乐阅读字典。不像我，瑞安两岁时就在看报纸了。

00:41:18.580 --> 00:41:33.250
<v Ira Glass>And his mom Angela told me he used to carry the cards from the trivial pursuit game she'd bought. One of his favorite childhood books was "Anguished English," an anthology of grammar humor. And, yes, Ryan loved Jeopardy.
<v Ira Glass>他妈妈安吉拉告诉我，他过去经常带着她买的冷知识追求游戏的卡片。他最喜欢的童书之一是《苦涩的英语》，这是一本语法幽默选集。是的，瑞安热爱《危险边缘》。

00:41:33.250 --> 00:41:43.310
<v Ryan Long>I would have loved to have met Alex Trebek. He was a hero in my house. He's one of few white men I know that can pull off a perm.
<v Ryan Long>我本来很想见见亚历克斯·特雷贝克。他是我家的英雄。他是少数几个我认识的能驾驭烫发的白人之一。

00:41:43.310 --> 00:41:50.580
<v Bim Adewunmi>[LAUGHS]
<v Bim Adewunmi>[笑]

00:41:50.580 --> 00:42:14.170
<v Ira Glass>Ryan, who's a big man now, describes himself as "husky" when he was a kid, which made him feel awkward and self-conscious. Plus, he was also in the midst of working out his mixed identity back then. His mom is Black and his father was white. And on top of that, he was an obviously smart kid. So they put him in the gifted classes, which also made him feel a little isolated.
<v Ira Glass>现在已经长大成人的瑞安描述自己小时候是“强壮的”，这让他感到尴尬和自我意识过剩。此外，他那时正处于理清自己混血身份的过程中。他妈妈是黑人，父亲是白人。最重要的是，他是个显然很聪明的孩子。所以他们把他放在天才班，这也让他感到有点孤立。

00:42:14.170 --> 00:42:22.050
<v Ryan Long>I was smart and stupid at the same time. I had no idea how to relate to people. I lived in my head.
<v Ryan Long>我既聪明又愚蠢。我不知道如何与人相处。我活在自己的脑子里。

00:42:22.050 --> 00:42:27.180
<v Bim Adewunmi>Uh-huh, which is good for accumulation of stuff, but--
<v Bim Adewunmi>嗯，这有利于积累东西，但——

00:42:27.180 --> 00:42:28.710
<v Ryan Long>Yeah.
<v Ryan Long>是的。

00:42:28.710 --> 00:42:29.700
<v Bim Adewunmi>--perhaps less useful--
<v Bim Adewunmi>——也许用处较小——

00:42:29.700 --> 00:42:31.730
<v Ryan Long>But not great for relating to other people.
<v Ryan Long>但不擅长与人相处。

00:42:31.730 --> 00:42:34.550
<v Bim Adewunmi>Uh-huh.
<v Bim Adewunmi>嗯。

00:42:34.550 --> 00:42:37.700
<v Ryan Long>I used to downplay my intelligence like a motherfucker.
<v Ryan Long>我过去经常像个混蛋一样贬低自己的智慧。

00:42:37.700 --> 00:42:37.960
<v Bim Adewunmi>Really?
<v Bim Adewunmi>真的吗？

00:42:37.960 --> 00:42:54.040
<v Ryan Long>Like crazy. I would just stay quiet. Because I would have an opinion on a topic or something like that, and I was afraid that it would go over the other kids' heads and they would look at me like I got three eyes or something. So I would just keep my mouth shut. You feel like you got to fit in.
<v Ryan Long>疯狂地。我只会保持安静。因为我对某个话题有看法或其他什么，我害怕它会超出其他孩子的理解能力，他们会看着我，就像我长了三只眼睛什么的。所以我会闭嘴。你觉得你必须合群。

00:42:54.040 --> 00:43:09.910
<v Bim Adewunmi>This was his first understanding that people have ideas about you, and those ideas might not match how you feel about yourself. It was also the first time Ryan felt put into that box that he tweeted about. And in order to fit, he had to cut parts of himself off.
<v Bim Adewunmi>这是他第一次理解人们对他有看法，而那些看法可能与他对自己的感觉不符。这也是瑞安第一次感到被放进他发推特提到的那个盒子里。为了合群，他不得不切掉自己的一部分。

00:43:09.910 --> 00:43:23.080
<v Bim Adewunmi>In the end, Ryan didn't do great at school. His parents had split up when he was 13, and he'd gone to live with his dad. A few years later, when he was 17, his father died and Ryan moved back in with his mom.
<v Bim Adewunmi>最终，瑞安在学校表现不佳。他父母在他13岁时离异，他随父亲生活。几年后，在他17岁时，父亲去世了，瑞安搬回去和妈妈住。

00:43:23.080 --> 00:43:44.830
<v Bim Adewunmi>According to Ryan, he barely graduated high school, and then did a year of community college before dropping out. It's like he got so used to hiding away the brightest parts of himself, that he got really good at it-- too good. The way Ryan tells it, he settled into the role of underachiever, and that made him prone to self-sabotage.
<v Bim Adewunmi>据瑞安说，他勉强高中毕业，然后在退学前读了一年社区学院。就像他习惯了隐藏自己最亮眼的部分，以至于变得非常擅长——太擅长了。按瑞安的说法，他陷入了落后者的角色，这让他容易自我毁灭。

00:43:44.830 --> 00:44:01.000
<v Ryan Long>It'll be something like a subconscious thing where I know I got an important meeting or something, I got to be somewhere. And my brain, I'll have this urge to just, "you know what, just roll over."
<v Ryan Long>这就像一种潜意识的东西，我知道我有一个重要的会议什么的，我必须去某个地方。我的大脑，我会有这种冲动，“你知道吗，就翻个身。”

00:44:01.000 --> 00:44:10.570
<v Ryan Long>Instead of getting up because I know it's important, it's like, "Just roll over. Whatever. It doesn't mean that much. You don't need this. It's not that important. You don't want it."
<v Ryan Long>而不是因为我知道它很重要而起床，它就像，“翻身吧。随便吧。这意义不大。你不需要这个。它没那么重要。你不想要它。”

00:44:10.570 --> 00:44:32.200
<v Ryan Long>And I'll oversleep, or I'll be late, or won't show up, something like that. It happens a lot, especially for things that could potentially benefit me. Because some part of me is either "this is stupid" or "you don't deserve it" or something. And I'm pretty sure that's depression.
<v Ryan Long>我会睡过头，或者迟到，或者不出现，诸如此类。这经常发生，特别是对于那些可能对我有利的事情。因为我的一部分要么是“这很蠢”，要么是“你不配”什么的。我很确定那是抑郁。

00:44:32.200 --> 00:44:53.560
<v Bim Adewunmi>For all the potential of his big brain, Ryan had the feeling that life was slipping him by. And when he became a father in his 30s, the question of lost potential became moot. He needed money to live and to provide, so he did all sorts of jobs-- airport security worker, warehouse grunt, package handler, office clerk.
<v Bim Adewunmi>拥有大脑袋的所有潜能，瑞安觉得生活正从他身边溜走。当他在30多岁成为父亲时，丢失潜能的问题就变得无关紧要了。他需要钱来生活和供养，所以他做了各种工作——机场安保员、仓库搬运工、包裹搬运工、办公室职员。

00:44:53.560 --> 00:45:06.220
<v Ryan Long>Piano mover, water ice guy, worked at UPS, was a cashier, was a bouncer, was a street sweeper for a couple of weeks. You got to do what you got to do, right?
<v Ryan Long>钢琴搬运工、水冰哥、在UPS工作过、做过收银员、保镖，做了几周的扫街工。你必须做你必须做的事，对吧？

00:45:06.220 --> 00:45:24.220
<v Bim Adewunmi>But then in January 2021, Ryan got sick with COVID. He had it really bad, and was in hospital for three weeks. He had to be given oxygen to stay alive. And he still has the photo of his saturated lungs, given to him by his doctor as a reminder of how close he came to death.
<v Bim Adewunmi>但后来在2021年1月，瑞安得了新冠。他病得很重，在医院住了三周。他不得不被吸氧以维持生命。他仍然保留着医生给他的饱和肺部照片，作为对他离死亡有多近的提醒。

00:45:24.220 --> 00:45:34.510
<v Bim Adewunmi>When he was discharged, he was a wreck. His knee gave out and he got diabetes. More than 20 years of the working grind seemed to catch up to him all at once.
<v Bim Adewunmi>当他出院时，他是一个残骸。他的膝盖不行了，他还得了糖尿病。20多年来的工作压力似乎一下子追上了他。

00:45:34.510 --> 00:45:50.620
<v Ryan Long>I felt in the moment like I'd wasted my life thus far. I hadn't done anything I wanted to do. I hadn't gone anywhere that I wanted to go. I started feeling like maybe I didn't have a huge amount of time left.
<v Ryan Long>那一刻我觉得我浪费了自己的一生。我没做过任何我想做的事。我没去过任何我想去的地方。我开始觉得也许我没有多少时间了。

00:45:50.620 --> 00:46:08.800
<v Ryan Long>My dad died young. His dad died young. I didn't want to have to do that to my kid. I found my dad dead. I thought, if you're going to have a memory of me, have a good one.
<v Ryan Long>我父亲死得早。他父亲死得早。我不想对我孩子那样。我发现我父亲死了。我想，如果你要对我有一个记忆，那就留一个好的。

00:46:08.800 --> 00:46:23.680
<v Bim Adewunmi>Getting COVID and surviving it was, in Ryan's own words, "a rockfall that turned into a landslide." It made him want to get out of his old habit of minimizing himself, this urge to snatch defeats from the jaws of victory.
<v Bim Adewunmi>得新冠并存活下来，用瑞安自己的话说，是“变成山崩的岩崩”。它让他想要摆脱贬低自己的旧习惯，这种从胜利的口中抢夺失败的冲动。

00:46:23.680 --> 00:46:35.080
<v Bim Adewunmi>That's when he got the call from Jeopardy. He'd taken the online test on a whim. And now, Ryan thought, this could be the thing he'd been hoping would come along to shake things up.
<v Bim Adewunmi>就在那时他接到了《危险边缘》的电话。他是一时兴起参加的在线测试。现在，瑞安想，这可能是他一直希望出现的改变现状的事情。

00:46:35.080 --> 00:46:57.770
<v Bim Adewunmi>That question I'd had about his journey to Jeopardy, it turns out the literal trip was a joint effort. He had about $200 to spare that month, and he had to make his own way to California. So he turned to his mom and his friends. He borrowed his mother's luggage and packed some black trousers plus two shirts-- one he'd bought himself, and the other bought by his mom.
<v Bim Adewunmi>对于他去《危险边缘》的旅程我有的那个问题，原来那次字面意义上的旅行是一次共同努力。那个月他只有大约200美元的闲钱，他必须自己去加利福尼亚。所以他求助于他的妈妈和朋友。他借了妈妈的行李箱，塞进了一些黑色长裤加上两件衬衫——一件是他自己买的，另一件是他妈妈买的。

00:46:57.770 --> 00:47:13.630
<v Bim Adewunmi>Remember how he made the decision as a teenager to hide his light under a bushel so he could be more normal? Now he was making another decision-- this time the guy whose tendency was to downplay his intelligence and his abilities was not going to get in his own way.
<v Bim Adewunmi>还记得他少年时做出的决定，隐藏自己的光芒以变得更正常吗？现在他又做了一个决定——这次，那个倾向于贬低自己智慧和能力的人不会再阻碍自己了。

00:47:13.630 --> 00:47:28.900
<v Ryan Long>The work was on me where I had to first acknowledge that I wanted it, give myself permission to want it. And say, OK, we're going to do this thing and we're going to do it right and we're not going to screw it up.
<v Ryan Long>工作在于我，我首先必须承认我想要它，给自己权限去想要它。并说，好的，我们要去做这件事，我们要把它做好，我们不会把它搞砸。

00:47:28.900 --> 00:47:44.530
<v Bim Adewunmi>He got on the plane to California. He'd never been off the east coast in his life. He was determined to enjoy himself. And he told himself, no expectations. Unlike some other wannabe winners, he did no tutorials on technique, no arduous studying.
<v Bim Adewunmi>他坐飞机去了加利福尼亚。他一生中从未走出过东海岸。他决心享受自己。他告诉自己，不要有期望。不像其他一些想赢的人，他没有做任何技术教程，没有艰苦的学习。

00:47:44.530 --> 00:48:01.390
<v Ryan Long>These are people that all year long they study, buying books on the buzzer technique and all that. When I've found out that people did that, [LAUGHS] I felt like, oh, should I have studied? I guess, maybe.
<v Ryan Long>这些人一年到头都在学习，购买关于蜂鸣器技术的书，所有这些。当我发现人们这样做时，[笑] 我觉得，哦，我应该学习吗？我想，也许吧。

00:48:01.390 --> 00:48:03.520
<v Bim Adewunmi>You didn't pick up "The Big Book of Jeopardy Questions?"
<v Bim Adewunmi>你没捡起《危险边缘问题大全》？

00:48:03.520 --> 00:48:10.330
<v Ryan Long>I didn't have time. I was working. I was working up until right before I left.
<v Ryan Long>我没时间。我在工作。我一直工作到离开前。

00:48:10.330 --> 00:48:30.790
<v Bim Adewunmi>On the day of his first game Ryan felt overwhelmed, but he worked through it. He let himself cry, which was an effective release valve for some of the emotional turmoil he was feeling. And then he pumped himself up with some music. Once he felt more in control, he headed to the studio to the show he'd watched since he was a little boy in Philly.
<v Bim Adewunmi>瑞安参加第一场比赛的那天感到不知所措，但他克服了。他让自己哭了出来，这是对他所感到的情感动荡的一种有效的释放阀。然后他用音乐振作自己。一旦他感觉更掌控局势，他就前往摄影棚，去他从费城还是个小男孩时就看的那个节目。

00:48:30.790 --> 00:48:47.220
<v Ryan Long>Felt like hallowed ground-- Jeopardy, the stage. When you get up there, it looks exactly like when Luke fought Vader on Bespin in "The Empire Strikes Back," the lighting.
<v Ryan Long>感觉像是圣地——《危险边缘》，那个舞台。当你站在上面时，它看起来就像卢克在《帝国反击战》中与维达在贝斯平战斗时那样，灯光效果。

00:48:47.220 --> 00:48:47.790
<v Bim Adewunmi>Oh, really?
<v Bim Adewunmi>哦，真的吗？

00:48:47.790 --> 00:48:58.340
<v Ryan Long>The red stair and then the blue background, that was the first thing I noticed when I got up there. I was like, oh, my god! Really felt like something, man.
<v Ryan Long>红色楼梯然后是蓝色背景，那是我站在上面时注意到的第一件事。我当时想，噢，天哪！真的感觉像是有什么东西，伙计。

00:48:58.340 --> 00:49:01.830
<v Mayim Bialik>--Sawyer and Tyrion Lannister. Ryan?
<v Mayim Bialik>——索耶和提利昂·兰尼斯特。瑞安？

00:49:01.830 --> 00:49:03.080
<v Ryan Long>Who is Peter Dinklage?
<v Ryan Long>彼得·丁拉基是谁？

00:49:03.080 --> 00:49:06.440
<v Mayim Bialik>Yes, and you just ran that category.
<v Mayim Bialik>是的，你刚刚跑完了那个分类。

00:49:06.440 --> 00:49:15.140
<v Bim Adewunmi>So that was a somewhat nerdy beginning of his 16-game streak. He puts the success down to two things-- knowing stuff and staying calm under pressure.
<v Bim Adewunmi>所以这是他16连胜的一个书呆子式的开始。他把成功归功于两件事——知道东西和在压力下保持冷静。

00:49:15.140 --> 00:49:15.470
<v Mayim Bialik>Ryan?
<v Mayim Bialik>瑞安？

00:49:15.470 --> 00:49:16.700
<v Ryan Long>Who was Jesse Owens?
<v Ryan Long>杰西·欧文斯是谁？

00:49:16.700 --> 00:49:17.450
<v Mayim Bialik>Correct.
<v Mayim Bialik>正确。

00:49:17.450 --> 00:49:37.070
<v Bim Adewunmi>In game mode, Ryan's demeanor was testament to the decision he'd made to go out there and just have fun. He laughed at himself when he flubbed the Daily Double, or realized he had bet too low on an easy answer. He delivered his cute little anecdotes in the "getting to know you" segments. But once the game was won, that easy grace was gone.
<v Bim Adewunmi>在比赛模式中，瑞安的举止证明了他为了去那里玩得开心而做出的决定。他会因为在每日双倍题中犯错或意识到自己对一个简单答案押注太低而嘲笑自己。他在“认识你”片段中讲述了他可爱的小轶事。但一旦比赛获胜，那种轻松的优雅就消失了。

00:49:37.070 --> 00:49:43.220
<v Mayim Bialik>Congratulations, you are our Jeopardy champion. Another fantastic week for Ryan Long.
<v Mayim Bialik>祝贺你，你是我们的《危险边缘》冠军。瑞安·朗又度过了精彩的一周。

00:49:43.220 --> 00:49:51.270
<v Bim Adewunmi>Instead, when his new total winnings were announced, he'd do that thing I loved. The head duck, and looking like he wanted the ground to swallow him up.
<v Bim Adewunmi>相反，当宣布他新的总奖金时，他会做那个我喜欢的动作。低头，看起来就像想让地把他吞掉。

00:49:51.270 --> 00:49:53.120
<v Ryan Long>I couldn't believe it I heard it every time.
<v Ryan Long>我无法相信我每次都听到它。

00:49:53.120 --> 00:49:53.810
<v Bim Adewunmi>Every time?
<v Bim Adewunmi>每次？

00:49:53.810 --> 00:50:10.120
<v Ryan Long>Every time I heard it, I was like, this-- nah. I don't think I could-- that didn't happen to me, [LAUGHS] you know?
<v Ryan Long>每次我听到它，我就想，这——不。我不认为我能——那没发生在我身上，[笑] 你知道吗？

00:50:10.120 --> 00:50:20.770
<v Brad Pearson>So I guess Ryan and I met on the very first day. Well, my only day of taping on Jeopardy, and Ryan's second day, I guess it was?
<v Brad Pearson>所以我猜我和瑞安在第一天就见了面。嗯，我参加《危险边缘》录制的一天，也是瑞安的第二天，我猜是？

00:50:20.770 --> 00:50:32.110
<v Bim Adewunmi>That's Brad Pearson. He was a challenger of Ryan's, also a Philly local. The two of them stayed friends after the show. They even had a little watch party at Brad's house the night his episode aired.
<v Bim Adewunmi>那是布拉德·皮尔逊。他是瑞安的挑战者，也是费城本地人。他们两人在节目后成了朋友。在这一集播出当晚，他们甚至在布拉德家举办了一个小型的观看派对。

00:50:32.110 --> 00:50:48.880
<v Bim Adewunmi>Over cheesesteaks, he told us about playing against Ryan in his fifth game. Contestants get to watch preceding games before they tape theirs. And all eyes were on Ryan, the returning champ, game after game. Brad was sitting with Tony, another of the pool of challengers.
<v Bim Adewunmi>吃着奶酪牛排，他告诉我们关于在第五场比赛中与瑞安对垒的情况。选手在录制前会观看之前的比赛。所有的目光都集中在瑞安身上，卫冕冠军，一场又一场。布拉德和挑战者池里的另一个人托尼坐在一起。

00:50:48.880 --> 00:50:54.990
<v Brad Pearson>As the day progressed, Tony turns to me at one point during the third game, and he goes, "He's only getting stronger!"
<v Brad Pearson>随着一天进展，托尼在第三场比赛中有一次转向我说，他说，“他越来越强了！”

00:50:54.990 --> 00:50:57.280
<v Bim Adewunmi>[LAUGHS]
<v Bim Adewunmi>[笑]

00:50:57.280 --> 00:51:08.860
<v Ira Glass>Brad noted the same thing I did-- that Ryan was a real all-rounder, banging out answers to everything from biblical locations, to the alumni of Saturday Night Live.
<v Ira Glass>布拉德注意到了我和一样的事情——瑞安是一个真正的全能选手，对从圣经地点到《周六夜现场》校友的一切问题都能回答出来。

00:51:08.860 --> 00:51:22.020
<v Brad Pearson>When I think of Ryan as a player, the thing is that you find certain people that are good at some really specific stuff. But Ryan's knowledge is so across the board, and also does really good on things that--
<v Brad Pearson>当我想到瑞安作为一名选手时，问题在于你会发现有些人擅长一些非常具体的东西。但瑞安的知识非常全面，而且在那些事情上也做得很好——

00:51:22.020 --> 00:51:23.590
<v Ryan Long>I got to go take a walk.
<v Ryan Long>我得出去走走。

00:51:23.590 --> 00:51:25.840
<v Brad Pearson>This is going to sound bad, but--
<v Brad Pearson>这听起来会很糟糕，但是——

00:51:25.840 --> 00:51:43.630
<v Bim Adewunmi>Ryan actually moved away when Brad was talking about him so admiringly. It's like the glare of praise was too bright for his eyes. He couldn't look directly at it. He did something similar when his mom bragged about him, and again when his friend Mari, who we also met, mentioned how great of a guy he was.
<v Bim Adewunmi>当布拉德如此赞赏地谈论他时，瑞安实际上走开了。就像赞美的刺眼光芒对他来说太亮了。他无法直视。当他妈妈吹嘘他，以及当我们见到的另一个朋友马里提到他是一个多么棒的人时，他也做了类似的动作。

00:51:43.630 --> 00:51:59.380
<v Bim Adewunmi>His face got all scrunched up, like it physically pained him to hear. But even so, over the course of his games, he beat contestants with far more impressive on-paper CVs. Even after winning, he knew that he wasn't seen as a typical Jeopardy winner.
<v Bim Adewunmi>他的脸缩成一团，好像听到这些让他身体上感到痛苦。但即便如此，在他比赛的过程中，他击败了那些纸面上履历令人印象深刻得多的选手。即使在获胜后，他也知道他没被视为典型的《危险边缘》赢家。

00:51:59.380 --> 00:52:07.710
<v Ryan Long>I don't look smart, whatever that means. I look like a bruiser.
<v Ryan Long>我不看起来聪明，不管那意味着什么。我看起来像个打手。

00:52:07.710 --> 00:52:08.860
<v Bim Adewunmi>Uh-huh.
<v Bim Adewunmi>嗯哼。

00:52:08.860 --> 00:52:28.760
<v Ryan Long>And people underestimate me a lot. I know I'm kind of smart. I know what I can do. There was a little tiny bit of class satisfaction. I don't mind admitting it--
<v Ryan Long>人们经常低估我。我知道我有点聪明。我知道我能做什么。有一点点阶级满足感。我不介意承认这一点——

00:52:28.760 --> 00:52:29.300
<v Bim Adewunmi>Yeah?
<v Bim Adewunmi>是吗？

00:52:29.300 --> 00:52:45.440
<v Ryan Long>--that I came in and beat all these wonderful people. People like my mom, people like me, we've always watched the show. We've just never been represented on the show before, I guess you could say. But we're out here.
<v Ryan Long>——我进来击败了所有这些了不起的人。像我妈妈那样的人，像我那样的人，我们一直都在看这个节目。我猜你可以说，之前我们从来没在节目中被代表过。但我们在这儿。

00:52:45.440 --> 00:53:01.490
<v Bim Adewunmi>Ryan's tweet thread about the box, he'd written that he didn't really believe a person could break out of the box. But, he added, I feel like this thing that just happened is proof that you can. And I wondered what he'd found outside of the box since he had managed to get out.
<v Bim Adewunmi>瑞安关于盒子的推文串，他写道他真的不相信一个人能跳出那个盒子。但，他补充说，我觉得刚刚发生的事情证明你可以。我想知道自从他成功跳出来后，他在盒子外面发现了什么。

00:53:01.490 --> 00:53:21.720
<v Bim Adewunmi>Returning to Philly, it was hard for Ryan to know how to act. His episodes wouldn't be on television for another few weeks. It was like he had to pretend something monumental hadn't just happened to him.
<v Bim Adewunmi>回到费城，瑞安很难知道该怎么做。他的节目在接下来的几周内不会在电视上播出。就像他必须假装什么重大事件没发生在他身上一样。

00:53:21.720 --> 00:53:37.560
<v Bim Adewunmi>There's no way to talk about Ryan's time on the show without talking about the money he won. $299,400 is quite the windfall. He no longer works as a rideshare driver, having paid off his old car and bought this new truck.
<v Bim Adewunmi>谈论瑞安在节目上的时间，没法不谈论他赢的钱。299,400美元可是一笔意外之财。他不再当拼车司机了，付清了旧车的钱，买了这辆新卡车。

00:53:37.560 --> 00:53:54.510
<v Bim Adewunmi>On the advice of his mom, he met up with a couple of people from a community improvement group for advice about what to do with this new earnings. Their advice was that he prioritize himself first, a concept that was completely foreign to him, and set up a chain reaction of self improvements.
<v Bim Adewunmi>在他妈妈的建议下，他见了社区改善小组的几个人，咨询如何处理这笔新收入。他们的建议是他优先考虑自己，这个概念对他来说完全陌生，并引发了一系列自我提升的连锁反应。

00:53:54.510 --> 00:54:18.070
<v Ryan Long>I wasn't a very reflective person, or anything like that, and I neglected myself for about 30 years. So I figured now is the time. I have a rare chance, which it seems like a lot of Americans don't have anymore. Because I got time to improve myself, take a real good look at myself, you know?
<v Ryan Long>我不是一个很善于反思的人，诸如此类，我忽视了自己大约30年。所以我想现在是时候了。我有一个难得的机会，这看起来很多美国人都没有了。因为我有时间提升自己，好好审视自己，你知道吗？

00:54:18.070 --> 00:54:32.620
<v Bim Adewunmi>Ryan started working out again. He got himself a therapist. He went digging in his family tree and found a book written by his third-time great grandfather, a staunch Methodist abolitionist. And he bought movies.
<v Bim Adewunmi>瑞安又开始锻炼了。他给自己找了个治疗师。他去挖掘家族树，发现了他第三代曾祖父写的书，一位坚定的卫理公会废奴主义者。他还买了电影。

00:54:32.620 --> 00:54:48.550
<v Ryan Long>I love, love, love films. I'll buy the extra special deluxe, whatever, whatever, if I can. So every July, The Criterion sale is 50% off, and every year something happens and I can't afford to go. And I was like, I'm going this year. [SNAPS] Going!
<v Ryan Long>我爱、爱、爱电影。如果可以，我会买超特别豪华版。所以每年七月，标准收藏（Criterion）销售半价，每年都会发生点什么，我买不起。我当时想，我今年要去。[响指] 要去！

00:54:48.550 --> 00:54:53.470
<v Bim Adewunmi>[LAUGHS] What's the first one you're going to watch, do you reckon?
<v Bim Adewunmi>[笑] 你认为你要看的第一部是什么？

00:54:53.470 --> 00:55:06.130
<v Ryan Long>Oh, you know, I think I'm going to watch Raging Bull. I heard The Criterion was coming out with the 4K, I was like, oh, my god, yes! It's so great. It's a great movie-- difficult to watch though.
<v Ryan Long>哦，你知道，我想我要看《愤怒的公牛》。我听说标准收藏要出4K版，我当时想，噢，天哪，是的！太棒了。这是一部伟大的电影——不过很难看。

00:55:06.130 --> 00:55:06.940
<v Bim Adewunmi>Horrible to watch--
<v Bim Adewunmi>很难看——

00:55:06.940 --> 00:55:07.210
<v Ryan Long>Yeah.
<v Ryan Long>是的。

00:55:07.210 --> 00:55:08.950
<v Bim Adewunmi>--in places.
<v Bim Adewunmi>——在某些地方。

00:55:08.950 --> 00:55:39.610
<v Ryan Long>Difficult, but it's also difficult because every man can recognize a part of himself in Jake LaMotta-- like some small part, some of us more than others. But we all have that ugly competitive sort of thing inside us, however small, or that ugly, self-loathing type beast.
<v Ryan Long>很难，但也因为每个男人都可以在杰克·拉莫塔身上认出自己的一部分——比如某些小部分，我们有些人比别人多。但我们内心都有那种丑陋的竞争性的东西，不管有多小，或者那种丑陋的、自我厌恶的野兽。

00:55:39.610 --> 00:55:54.980
<v Bim Adewunmi>Getting to finally buy Criterion movies, working on his LaMotta style self-loathing, spending hours researching family history, the whole "Project Ryan," it's being facilitated by the most precious commodity his Jeopardy run brought him.
<v Bim Adewunmi>终于可以买标准收藏电影，研究拉莫塔式的自我厌恶，花几个小时研究家庭历史，整个“瑞安计划”，正被《危险边缘》带来的最珍贵的商品所促进。

00:55:54.980 --> 00:55:58.860
<v Bim Adewunmi>So, clearly, Jeopardy changed your life. So what is your life now?
<v Bim Adewunmi>那么，显然，《危险边缘》改变了你的生活。那么你现在的生活是什么？

00:55:58.860 --> 00:56:05.770
<v Ryan Long>It allowed me the opportunity to have time to do this, which I wouldn't have had time to do anyway.
<v Ryan Long>它给了我机会拥有时间去做这件事，这是我无论如何都没时间做的。

00:56:05.770 --> 00:56:07.080
<v Zoe Chace>What's this?
<v Zoe Chace>什么是这？

00:56:07.080 --> 00:56:26.760
<v Ryan Long>It-- examine myself, I guess. Just everything before, looking where I come from and what makes me tick. It's a valuable thing. I think everybody needs to do it, but not everybody has time to do it.
<v Ryan Long>审视我自己，我猜。之前发生的一切，看看我来自哪里以及是什么驱动了我。这是一件宝贵的事情。我觉得每个人都需要这样做，但不是每个人都有时间这样做。

00:56:26.760 --> 00:56:32.980
<v Bim Adewunmi>So that's the biggest post-Jeopardy change is just an abundance of time for you now?
<v Bim Adewunmi>所以《危险边缘》后最大的变化只是现在你拥有了大量时间？

00:56:32.980 --> 00:56:39.180
<v Ryan Long>Yeah. That's the most valuable thing that I got out of this.
<v Ryan Long>是的。这是我从中得到的最大的宝藏。

00:56:39.180 --> 00:56:41.100
<v Bim Adewunmi>Not the money?
<v Bim Adewunmi>不是钱？

00:56:41.100 --> 00:56:50.820
<v Ryan Long>Well, the money made the time possible. They say money can't buy happiness, but it buys everything that makes you happy-- even time.
<v Ryan Long>好吧，钱使时间成为可能。人们说钱买不到幸福，但它买到了所有让你快乐的东西——甚至是时间。

00:56:50.820 --> 00:56:54.840
<v Ryan Long>[MUSIC PLAYING]
<v Ryan Long>[音乐播放]

00:56:54.840 --> 00:57:34.880
<v Bim Adewunmi>I had come to Philly to talk to Ryan about navigating outside expectations as they crashed up against what he thought about himself, and I got that. But I also got this accidental reflection on American capitalism, and how so many of us are served so poorly by the way life is currently set up as all work and no time. Jeopardy provided a very specific Ryan-shaped escape hatch. It gave him the space to stare into that crack between realities, and fish out all the elements of what his life could be.
<v Bim Adewunmi>我来费城和瑞安谈论在外部期望与他对自己看法碰撞时的应对，我得到了答案。但我也得到了关于美国资本主义的意外反思，以及我们中有多少人被生活目前设置成全工作没时间的方式服务得如此糟糕。 《危险边缘》提供了一个非常具体的瑞安形状的逃生舱。它给了他盯着现实之间裂缝的机会，并钓出他生活可能是什么样的所有元素。

00:57:34.880 --> 00:57:59.970
<v Ira Glass>Bim Adewunmi is one of the producers of our show.
<v Ira Glass>比姆·阿德温米是我们节目的制作人之一。

